1 NOTE (Seleucus Callinicus), d. 226 BC, king of ancient Syria (247-226 BC), sonof Antiochus II. On his father's death there was a struggle for thethrone between Seleucus and his stepmother, Berenice (on behalf of herinfant son). Seleucu
2 CONC s seems to have murdered both Berenice and her sonbefore her brother Ptolemy III of Egypt could arrive. A long war withPtolemy ensued. Seleucus also had to wage war with his own brother,Antiochus Hierax, for Asia Minor. Bactria an
2 CONC d Parthia revolted and threwoff Seleucid control. He was succeeded by his son Seleucus III, who waskilled after a three-year reign; another son, Antiochus III , then becameking.
2 CONT
2 CONT Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Edition, Copyright (c) 2003.
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT Seleucus II was a weak leader and soon involved in several disastrousbattles. Ptolemy III campaigned in Palestine, then continued north andconquered Antioch, the capital of the Seleucid Empire. Ptolemy IIIcontinued to the Euphrate
2 CONC s River where Seleucus II finally held him.Meanwhile, Seleucus was also occupied with internal revolts. The PersianEmpire had begun to break away, beginning with Bactria [Afghanistan],then Parthia [Iran].
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of his surname meaning gloriously victorious, he lost thebattles, and the empire shrunk to its lowest. He was succeeded by his sonSeleucus III.
