2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy V Epiphanes (Greek: Πτολεμαῖος Ἐπιφανής, Ptolemaĩos Epiphanḗs, reigned 204–181 BCE), son of Ptolemy IV Philopator and Arsinoe III of Egypt, was the 5th ruler of the Ptolemaic dynasty. He became ruler at the age of five, an
2 CONC d under a series of regents the kingdom was paralyzed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regency infighting
2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy Epiphanes was only a small boy when his father, Ptolemy Philopator, died. The two leading favorites of Philopator, Agathocles and Sosibius, fearing that Arsinoe would secure the regency had her murdered before she heard of
2 CONC  her husband's death, which secured the regency for themselves. In 202 BCE however Tlepolemus, the general in charge of Pelusium, put himself at the head of a revolt. Once Epiphanes was in the hands of Tlepolemus he was persuaded 
2 CONC to give a sign that the killers of his mother should be killed. According to Bevan the child king's consent was given more from fear than anything else and Agathocles along with several of his supporters being killed by the Alexan
2 CONC drian mob.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT War with Egypt and Macedonia
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus III the Great and Philip V of Macedon made a pact to divide the Ptolemaic possessions overseas. Philip seized several islands and places in Caria and Thrace, whilst the Battle of Panium (198 BCE) definitely transferred C
2 CONC oele-Syria, including Judea, from the Ptolemies to the Seleucids.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus after this concluded peace, giving his own daughter Cleopatra I to Epiphanes to marry (193–192 BCE). Nevertheless, when war broke out between Antiochus and Rome, Egypt ranged itself with the latter power. Epiphanes in ma
2 CONC nhood was remarkable as a passionate sportsman; he excelled in athletic exercises and the chase.
2 CONT
2 CONT Struggle against the Egyptian Revolt
2 CONT
2 CONT Great cruelty and treachery were displayed in the suppression of the native rebellion, and some accounts represent him as personally tyrannical. In 197 BCE Lycopolis was held by the forces of Ankmachis, (also known as Chaonnophris
2 CONC ) the secessionist pharaoh of Upper Egypt, but was forced to withdraw to Thebes. The war between North and South continued until 185 BCE with the arrest of Ankmachis by Ptolemaic General Conanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 183 BCE/184 BCE The rebels in Lower Egypt surrendered on the basis of terms that Epiphanes had given his personal to honour. However, showing himself in the opinion of Bevan treacherous and vindictive he had them put to death i
2 CONC n a cruel manner.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rosetta Stone was a statement of thanks to the Egyptian priesthood for help during the crisis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT The elder of his two sons, Ptolemy VI Philometor (181–145 BCE), succeeded as an infant under the regency of his mother Cleopatra the Syrian. Her death was followed by a rupture between the Ptolemaic and Seleucid courts, on the old
2 CONC  question of Coele-Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ptolomeo V Epífanes n.210 -+ 181 AC  Fue rey de Egipto desde los catorce años. Pertenece a la dinastía ptolemaica. Fue el último de los grandes reyes lágidas, tras él las luchas dinásticas y civiles caracterizaron el fin de la din
2 CONC astía ptolomeica.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Ptolemy V Epiphanes=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy V Epiphanes (Ancient Greek: Πτολεμαῖος Ἐπιφανής, Ptolemaĩos Epiphanḗs, reigned 204–181 BC), son of Ptolemy IV Philopator and Arsinoe III of Egypt, was the fifth ruler of the Ptolemaic dynasty. He inherited the throne at th
2 CONC e age of five, and under a series of regents, the kingdom was paralyzed. The Rosetta Stone was produced during his reign as an adult.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Regency infighting''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy Epiphanes was only a small boy when his father, Ptolemy Philopator, died. Philopator's two leading favorites, Agathocles and Sosibius, fearing that Arsinoe would secure the regency, had her murdered before she heard of her
2 CONC  husband's death, thereby securing the regency for themselves. However, in 202 BC, Tlepolemus, the general in charge of Pelusium, put himself at the head of a revolt. Once Epiphanes was in the hands of Tlepolemus he was persuaded 
2 CONC to give a sign that his mother's killers should be killed. The child king gave his consent, it is thought more from fear than anything else, and Agathocles along with several of his supporters were killed by the Alexandrian mob.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''War with Egypt and Macedonia''
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus III the Great and Philip V of Macedon made a pact to divide the Ptolemaic possessions overseas. Philip seized several islands and populated places in Caria and Thrace, whilst the Battle of Panium (198 BC) definitively tr
2 CONC ansferred Coele-Syria, including Judea, from the Ptolemies to the Seleucids.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus then concluded peace, giving his own daughter Cleopatra I to Epiphanes in marriage (193–192 BC). Nevertheless, when war broke out between Antiochus and Rome, Egypt ranged itself with the latter power.
2 CONT
2 CONT In manhood, Epiphanes was a passionate sportsman; he excelled in athletic exercises and the chase.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The Egyptian Revolt''
2 CONT
2 CONT Great cruelty and treachery were displayed in the suppression of the native rebellion, and some accounts represent Epiphanes as personally tyrannical. In 197 BC, Lycopolis was held by the forces of Ankmachis (also known as Chaonno
2 CONC phris), the secessionist pharaoh of Upper Egypt, but he was forced to withdraw to Thebes. The war between North and South continued until 185 BC with the arrest of Ankmachis by Ptolemaic General Conanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 183 BC/184 BC, the rebels in Lower Egypt surrendered on the basis of terms that Epiphanes had personally promised to honor. However, showing himself treacherous and vindictive, he had them put to death in a cruel manner.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Memphis Decree, published in three languages on the Rosetta Stone and other stelae, announced the rule and ascension to godhood of Ptolemy V, and contained concessions to the priesthood, and has been termed a reward for the pr
2 CONC iests' support.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Succession'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The elder of Ptolemy V's two sons, Ptolemy VI Philometor (181–145 BC), succeeded as an infant under the regency of his mother Cleopatra the Syrian. Her death was followed by a rupture between the Ptolemaic and Seleucid courts, on 
2 CONC the old question of Coele-Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Legacy'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy V's reign was also marked by trade with other contemporaneous polities. In the 1930s, excavations by Mattingly at a fortress close to Port Dunford (the likely Nikon of antiquity) in present-day southern Somalia yielded a n
2 CONC umber of Ptolemaic coins. Among these pieces were 17 copper mints from the Ptolemy III to Ptolemy V dynasties, as well as late Imperial Rome and Mamluk Sultanate coins.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemy_V_Epiphanes
