1 NOTE Ptolemy VI (186?-145 bc), called Ptolemy Philometor ("loving his mother"), 
2 CONT king of Egypt (181-145 bc), the son of Ptolemy V and Cleopatra I. During 
2 CONT his reign, Egypt was invaded by the Seleucid king Antiochus IV, and 
2 CONT Ptolemy was made a prisoner. The throne was then given by the Alexandrians 
2 CONT to his brother, Ptolemy VII. After Antiochus withdrew, the brothers ruled 
2 CONT as joint kings. Conflicts between them arose, and through Roman 
2 CONT arbitration, Cyrenaica was given to Ptolemy VII to rule. After the death 
2 CONT of the Seleucid king Demetrius I in 150 bc, Ptolemy VI was offered the 
2 CONT Seleucid crown by the people of Antioch, but he declined in favor of the 
2 CONT legitimate heir, Demetrius II. In 145 bc Ptolemy VI died of wounds 
2 CONT received in a battle, which resulted in the final defeat of Demetrius II's 
2 CONT rival, Alexander Balas (died 145 bc).
