2 FORM png
2 TITL Mithridates VI of Pontus - Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 NOTE Mithradates VI (Mithradates Eupator) (mthrkd´tz) (KEY) , c.131 B.C.-63B.C., king of Pontus, sometimes called Mithradates the Great. He extendedhis empire until, in addition to Pontus, he held Cappadocia, Paphlagonia,and the Black 
2 CONC Sea coast beyond the Caucasus. The increasing importance ofRome in Asia Minor brought Mithradates and the republic into openconflict. The First Mithradatic War (88 B.C.-84 B.C.) was the result.Mithradates conquered the whole of As
2 CONC ia Minor (except for a few cities)in 88 B.C. In 85 B.C. the Roman general Fimbria attacked him in AsiaMinor, and he was defeated simultaneously with the destruction of hisarmy in Greece. In the resultant treaty Mithradates paid an
2 CONC  indemnity andgave up all but Pontus and a few colonies. The Second Mithradatic War (83B.C.-81 B.C.) was begun by Sulla’s lieutenant Lucius Murena, who desiredglory. Murena was repelled by Mithradates and was superseded by AulusGa
2 CONC binius, who made peace with the king of Pontus. The Third MithradaticWar (74 B.C.-63 B.C.) began when Mithradates resolved to prevent Romefrom annexing Bithynia, which had been left to Rome by a royal will.Lucullus was sent agains
2 CONC t Mithradates, who was finally forced to flee toArmenia. In 68 B.C. the Romans invaded Armenia, but were forced toretreat. Mithradates returned to Pontus, and Lucullus was replaced (66B.C.) by Pompey. Pompey soon drove Mithradates
2 CONC  eastward, and the kingfled to the Crimea, the last of his provinces. He had a slave kill him.His fall is the subject of Racine’s Mithridate. Pharnaces II was his sonand Tigranes, his son-in-law. The name is also spelled Mithridat
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Edition. Copyright © 2002 ColumbiaUniversity Press
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT Mithridates
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates while still a child developed a reputation that includedmatricide and a paranoid fear of being poisoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT --I tell the tale that I heard told.
2 CONT Mithridates, he died old.
2 CONT From A.E. Housman "Terence, this is stupid stuff"
2 CONT
2 CONT Review and summary:
2 CONT Both Sulla and Marius wanted the honor of disposing of the greatestchallenge to Roman supremacy since Hannibal Barca -- the long-livedMithridates VI of Pontus (132-63 B.C.) who was a thorn in Rome's side forforty years. The rivalr
2 CONC y of the two generals led to loss of Roman blood-- at home (although Sulla also confronted Mithridates).
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite competence that matched the rival generals' confidence in theirability to check the Eastern despot, it was Pompey the Great who disposedof the Mithridatic problem.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mountainous district of Pontus lay on the eastern side of the BlackSea, beyond the province of Asia and Bithynia, north of Galatia andCappadocia, west of Armenia, and south of Colchis. In the Third PunicWar, King Mithridates E
2 CONC uergetes (who claimed descent from the PersianKing Darius) of Pontus helped Rome. Phrygia Maior was given him ingratitude. By the time Rome had annexed Pergamum to create the provinceof Asia (129 B.C.), Pontus was ruled from its c
2 CONC apital in Sinope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates - Youth and Poison
2 CONT
2 CONT In 120, while still a child, Mithridates Eupator (132-83 B.C.) becameking. His mother (said to have assassinated her husband Mithridates V)ruled in her young son's stead. Afraid his mother would try to kill him,Mithridates went in
2 CONC to hiding, at which time he started ingesting smalldoses of various poisons in order to develop an immunity. WhenMithridates returned (c. 115-111), he took command, had his motherimprisoned (and, possibly, executed), and set about
2 CONC  extending hisdominion. After he acquired Greek towns in Colchis and what's now theCrimea, Mithridates established a strong fleet to hold his territories.Since the Greeks provided revenue, officers, and mercenary soldiers, heset o
2 CONC ut to extend his Greek resources.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 108/07 Mithridates and Nicomedes II of Bithynia marched intoPaphlagonia. A Roman emisssary tried to force Nicomedes and Mithridatesto restore its king, Astreodon. Instead, Mithridates annexed a piece ofGalatia which his father 
2 CONC was supposed to have inherited from earlierrulers, while Nicomedes, far from restoring Paphlagonia to Astreodon, puthis son on the throne as puppet. The unsuccessful Roman embassy went home.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 102 Mithridates waged war on Cappadocia and installed hiseight-year-old son as king under the regency of a man named Gordius.Mithridates tried to bribe the Roman senators to accept this arrangement,his presence in Paphlagonia a
2 CONC nd Galatia, and to counter Nicomedes' claimto Cappadocia. At the same time, Nicomedes appealed to Rome on behalf ofhis own pretender. Instead of taking a clear side, Rome orderedMithridates to get out of Cappadocia and Nicomedes t
2 CONC o leave Paphlagonia.Mithridates obliged and, as he claimed in 89 B.C., he also leftPaphlagonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Left to their own devices, the Cappadocian nobility selected Ariobarzanesfor their king. Sulla helped Ariobarzanes assume power.
2 CONT
2 CONT By allying himself with the neighboring kingdom through marriage,Mithridates hoped to expand his empire. The Armenians would do theinvading and reap the spoils while Pontus would gain the territory. Inthis way, Mithridates himself
2 CONC  would not antagonize the Romans.
2 CONT In 96/5 Tigranes I inherited the throne of Armenia and marriedMithridates' daughter Cleopatra. In 94, Nicomedes of Bithynia died. By91, Rome was embroiled in its own Social War, leaving Mithridates anopportunity to expand his empi
2 CONC re. He easily persuaded his son-in-law totake Cappadocia, offering to divide the spoils. Next, Mithridates wentafter Nicomedes' successor in Bithynia, Nicomedes III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ousted rulers appealed to Rome which ruled that they should berestored. M. Aquilius and Manlius Maltinus were ordered to deal with themonarchs of Pontus and Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT Greedy Aquilius suggested a course of action that led to the FirstMithridatic War and his own ironic execution. Aquilius suggested thatsince the Roman proteges in Bithynia and Cappadocia were unable to paytheir debt to Rome, they 
2 CONC should invade Mithridates' kingdom. To oblige,Nicomedes invaded Pontic territory and closed off the Bithynian Black Seaexit to Pontic ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates' response was to turn to the Romans for help, but he wasrebuffed, so he sent his son Ariarathus into Cappadocia to driveAriobazares out. Mithridates then sent an envoy to the Romancommissioners. The Romans accused the 
2 CONC envoy of threatening war, and sobegan the first Mithridatic War -- without the ratification of the Senateand the Roman people.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates fought back. Since the Roman troops were not prepared,Mithridates saw that he could push into the Roman province of Asia. Hedid so, won, killed Aquilius (see below), and ordered the massacre of all(80,000 or, according
2 CONC  to Plutarch, 150,000) Italians throughout the area.It is suggested that this was to insure loyalty, because all thoseinvolved in the massacre would have had much to dread from Romanvengeance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates' reputation for cruelty may not have been deserved. He couldalso be merciful and generous with the people he conquered.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some cities that surrendered to Mithridates were rewarded with taxrelief. Amnesty was offered those who handed over the Roman generals.Aquilius had fled to the island of Lesbos, but its citizens wantingamnesty, handed him over. Un
2 CONC like the leniency with which Mithridates hadtreated another Roman general, Oppius, whom he set free, Aquilius wasridiculed and then forced to endure an ironic death. Since it was hisgreed that had set the war in motion, Mithridate
2 CONC s had molten gold pouredinto Aquilius' mouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates then attempted to add Greece to his territory. He encounteredlittle resistance from Greeks tired of the tax-farming Roman equites. Bythe spring of 87, Mithridates controlled almost all of Asia Minor,Greece, Thrace, par
2 CONC t of Macedon, and dominated the Black Sea and Aegean,with the help of his general, Archelaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally free from pressing business at Rome Sulla left for Greece andAsia Minor where he easily stopped Mithridates' aggression.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was at the beginning of 87 that Sulla was able to leave Italy, onceagain, for Greece, where he began a string of successful campaigns. For awhile the Greeks remained loyal to the Pontic king, but when it becameclear the Romans 
2 CONC might be able to restore the old order, the Greekschanged sides, preferring usurious Roman tax-collectors to an Orientaldespot.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to The Treaty of Dardanus at the conclusion of the FirstMithridatic War, Mithridates was still a friend of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Romans captured Mithridates' headquarters in Pergamum,Mithridates was ready to make peace. Since Sulla wished to return to Rometo deal with his opponents there, the terms of the treaty (Treaty ofDardanus, 85/4 B.C.) were
2 CONC  not as terrible for Mithridates as they wouldhave been otherwise. Mithridates retained Pontus, the rest of histerritories, his status as ally of Rome, but had to abandon his part ofPaphlagonia and Asia, restore Bithynia to Nicome
2 CONC des and Cappadocia toAriobarzanes, return his prisoners, and pay 2-3000 talents as warindemnity and provide Sulla with 70-80 fully equipped ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT 83-63 B.C.
2 CONT When Mithridates failed to live up to his agreement to leave Cappadocia,a second Mithridatic War broke out in 83. Propraetor Murena invadedPontus but was forced to withdraw. In 81, peace was declared followingthe terms of the trea
2 CONC ty of Dardanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of the rebellion of Spartacus, Rome sent in Lucius LiciniusLucullus and Aurelius Cotta to deal with Mithridates. Lucullus' troopswere in no shape to fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 75 or 74 Nicomedes III of Bithynia died, bestowing his kingdom uponRome. Threatened by the power of Rome in such proximity, Mithridates tookhis largest force into the neighboring kingdom, occupied Bithynia, andsent smaller forc
2 CONC es against Asia, Cappadocia and Phrygia. In return, torepel Mithridates from Asia, the Roman senate sent Lucius LiciniusLucullus. Marcus Aurelius Cotta was sent with a fleet to occupy Bithynia.When Lucullus arrived he found troops
2 CONC  in need of discipline. Trainingthem kept him from helping Cotta, making it easy for Mithridates todefeat Cotta at Chalcedon and then beseige Cyzicus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally equipped with barely disciplined troops, Lucullus severedMithridates' land communication, while the citizens of Cyzicus held outagainst the seige long enough for Mithridates to change his mind andwithdraw.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to The Treaty of Dardanus at the conclusion of the FirstMithridatic War, Mithridates was still a friend of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Romans captured Mithridates' headquarters in Pergamum,Mithridates was ready to make peace. Since Sulla wished to return to Rometo deal with his opponents there, the terms of the treaty (Treaty ofDardanus, 85/4 B.C.) were
2 CONC  not as terrible for Mithridates as they wouldhave been otherwise. Mithridates retained Pontus, the rest of histerritories, his status as ally of Rome, but had to abandon his part ofPaphlagonia and Asia, restore Bithynia to Nicome
2 CONC des and Cappadocia toAriobarzanes, return his prisoners, and pay 2-3000 talents as warindemnity and provide Sulla with 70-80 fully equipped ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mithridates tended to retreat when the Romans showed force. When Lucullusdrove into Mithridates' kingdom of Pontus, Mithridates fled to Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once again, Mithridates decided to retreat. Lucullus did not immediatelypursue him, but instead gathered a fleet from the cities in Asia. Then,in 73, with his new fleet he almost destoyed Mithridates. Lucullus wentinto Pontus to f
2 CONC ight Mithridates at Cabira. Routed, Mithridates fled toArmenia looking for help from his son-in-law Tigranes. The Armenian kingwas not willing to provide military aid, but only shelter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tigranes may not have been anxious to fight the Romans, but he wasn'tabout to surrender his father-in-law, Mithridates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, unopposed, Lucullus captured almost the entire kingdom ofPontus. He then ordered Tigranes to surrender Mithridates. When Tigranesrefused, Lucullus attacked Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucullus' main obstacles, at this time, were his own soldiers who refusedfurther campaigns. This mutiny gave Mithridates the chance to take backsome of the territory he had lost. Realizing they needed another leader,the Romans sen
2 CONC t Pompey to Asia.
2 CONT
2 CONT When properly equipped with disciplined troops and a competent general,there was no contest between Rome and Pontus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey began invading Pontus in 66 B.C. Mithridates tried unsuccessfullyto enlist the support of Tigranes, but his brother-in-law had troubles ofhis own. Tigranes's son, backed by Rome, was seeking his father's throne.Without his 
2 CONC ally, Mithridates fled to his possessions in the Crimea.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that when Mithridates saw that the people of the Crimeasupported his son over him, Mithridates endeavored to take his own life,but failed because of the resistance he'd built up to poison. He had toask one of his mercen
2 CONC ary soldiers to kill him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey (named Magnus, the Great, for his victory over Mithridates) hadMithridates' corpse buried honorably in the royal tomb at Sinope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT H. H. Scullard's revised version of F.B. Marsh's Roman World 146-30 B.C.
2 CONT Cambridge Ancient History Vol. IX, 1994.
2 CONT http://ancienthistory.about.com/library/weekly/aa053000a.htm
