1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Tishyaraksha=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tishyaraksha (died c. 238 BC) was the last wife of the third Mauryan emperor, Ashoka. According to the Ashokavadana, she was responsible for blinding Ashoka's son and heir presumptive Kunala.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Tishyaraksha was a favourite maid of Ashoka's chief queen, Asandhimitra, and after her mistress died, she went to Pataliputra becoming a great dancer and charmed Ashoka with her dance and beauty. Later, she bec
2 CONC ame his concubine and during the later life of Ashoka she took care of his health as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Kunala''
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also believed that due to the age difference between her and Ashoka, she was attracted towards Kunala, a son of Ashoka who was religious in nature and inclined towards Jainism from early on. Kunala regarded Tishyaraksha as h
2 CONC is mother due to her place in the Mauryan Empire at the time. After perceiving rejection from Kunala, Tishyaraksha turned so furious that she decided to blind him. It is believed that the eyes of Kunala were attractive and beautif
2 CONC ul and that they had originally attracted Tishyaraksha to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Plot'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Chandragupta Sabha led by Radhagupta (the then minister (Mahaamatya) of the Mauryan Empire) decided that Kunala would proceed to subjugate the revolt of Takshashila (Taxila), Tishyaraksha conceived a plot. The plot succee
2 CONC ded after the conquest by Kunala.
2 CONT
2 CONT As per the plot, Ashoka had to request two very precious jewels from the governor of Takshashila which were believed to have been the most unusual of their kind. The decisive language of the letter written by Tishyaraksha was sent
2 CONC  by Ashoka who did not understand the hidden meaning and therefore could not explain it to Kunala. However, Kunala immediately understood the hidden meaning, but due to his love for his father and his loyalty towards Magadha, he f
2 CONC elt forced to remove his own eyes. Then he sent both of his eyes to the court of Magadha at Pataliputra. Ashoka realized his fault but by then it was too late. Immediately Radhagupta ordered Tishyaraksha's death. However, it is be
2 CONC lieved that Tishyaraksha committed suicide after finding out this news.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In popular culture'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Haraprasad Shastri's second novel " Kanchanmala" features Tisshyaraksha in a prominent role. The story of Tishyaraksha has also been captured by the Bengali writer Samaresh Majumdar in his novel "Saranagata", however, with very di
2 CONC fferent strokes and shades that are attributed to the life of Ashoka.The same line of story was developed by a prominent Bengali dramatist Amit Maitra into a drama titled 'Dharmashok'.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tishyaraksha
