1 NOTE Ptolemy I (367?-283 bc), called Ptolemy Soter ("preserver"), king of Egypt 
2 CONT (323-285 bc), founder of the Ptolemaic dynasty. The son of Lagus, a 
2 CONT Macedonian of common birth, Ptolemy was a general in the army of Alexander 
2 CONT the Great and took a leading part in Alexander's later campaigns in Asia. 
2 CONT On the death of Alexander in 323 bc, his empire was divided among the 
2 CONT Diadochi (successors) by the imperial regent Perdiccas (365-321 bc) and 
2 CONT Ptolemy was appointed satrap of Egypt and Libya. He was from the first an 
2 CONT independent ruler, engaging in long wars with other Macedonian chiefs in 
2 CONT order to secure and extend his rule. Ptolemy was prevented from holding 
2 CONT Cyprus and parts of Greece, but he resisted invasions of Egypt and Rhodes 
2 CONT and occupied Palestine and Cyrenaica. In 305 bc he assumed the title of 
2 CONT king. Alexandria was his capital, and he founded the famous Alexandrian 
2 CONT library. He was the author of a lost history of the campaigns of 
2 CONT Alexander. In 285 bc Ptolemy I abdicated in favor of one of his younger 
2 CONT sons.
1 NOTE The generals who succeed Alexander are Antigonus Cyclops orMonophthalmus, so-called because he lost an eye in battle, and his sonDemetrius Poliocertes, Antipater and his son Cassander, Seleucus,Ptolemy, Eumenes and Lysimachus. The
2 CONC y argue bitterly among themselves foreach is determined to build a Hellenistic or Greek monarchy on the ruinsof Alexander's empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy, son of a Macedonian nobleman and the most trusted of Alexander'sgenerals, was among the seven bodyguards attached to his person. In thedivision of the empire, Ptolemy takes Egypt as the safest and farthestplace to establi
2 CONC sh a dynasty. He even manages to carry off the body ofAlexander from Babylon to Egypt in order to bury him in Alexandria andthus enhance his own position.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Ptolemy mints a gold coin at Alexandria on which we see a car drawnby four elephants. Perhaps this is an attempt made by him to representAlexander's funeral cortege that included elephants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antipater establishes himself in Macedon. He dies soon after and issucceeded by Cassander, his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seleucus Nicator, a youth of twenty-three of age when he accompaniesAlexander to Asia, wins distinction in the Indian campaign. Seleucus isgiven the government of the Babylonian satrapy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antigonus defeats Eumenes, installed as satrap of Cappadocia, and has himput to death. He thus gets rid of his most dangerous rival. OstensiblyAntigonus and his son Demetrius Poliocertes hope to reunify Alexander'scollapsing empir
2 CONC e but for their own purposes. Antigonus also controlsparts of Greece, Asia Minor and Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lysimachus sets himself up in Thrace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military clashes eventually occur as each tries to encroach on theother's territory. Ptolemy annexes Phoenicia to his possessions andplaces garrisons in the Phoenician port cities. Antigonus too decides toenlarge his territory and
2 CONC  set himself up as king of Asia Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning from successful wars in Babylonia, Antigonus easily takes overthe cities of Phoenicia but meets with firm resistance from Tyre.Seventeen years have passed since Alexander took Tyre and the city hasrecovered rapidly. Anti
2 CONC gonus has few ships as Ptolemy is holding allPhoenician vessels and their crews in Egypt, so he decides to build afleet of his own. He camps before Tyre, summons all the kings of thePhoenician cities and the viceroys of Syria and 
2 CONC demands them to assisthim in building ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antigonus blockades Tyre by land. He establishes three shipyards, one atTripolis, one at Byblos, one at Sidon. Diodorus Siculus records thatAntigonus collects wood-cutters, sawyers and shipwrights from all regionsand has wood carr
2 CONC ied from the mountains of Lebanon to the sea. Eightthousand men are employed to cut and saw the timber; one thousand pairsof draught animals are used to transport it. "This mountain range",Diodorus (19.58.3-5) writes, "extends alo
2 CONC ng the territory of Tripolis,Byblos and Sidon and is covered with cedar and cypress trees of wonderfulbeauty and size." We thus have a description of the extent of theluxuriant forests covering the mountains of Lebanon about two t
2 CONC housandthree hundred years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a siege of fifteen months, Tyre is taken by Antigonus. He allowsPtolemy's garrison to leave and establishes his own in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to enhance their personal prestige, Alexander's successorsstrike their own coins. On the obverse of his early silver coinage,Ptolemy has engraved the head of the newly deified Alexander with thesacred ram's horns of Ammon
2 CONC  and an elephant headdress. Alexander's name,not his, appears on the reverse of his coins.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the coins of Seleucus, Alexander is portrayed as the god Dionysuswearing a helmet covered with panther skin adorned with a bull's ear andhorns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lysimachus in his turn presents on his coins the diademed head ofAlexander, deified, wearing the sacred horns of Ammon. When Alexanderconquered Egypt, he was hailed by the high-priest of Ammon as the son ofthe god and Alexander's 
2 CONC generals are determined to let no one forget it.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 305 B.C. Antigonus and his son Demetrius assume the title of king.Ptolemy, Cassander, Lysimachus and Seleucus react to the challenge bydoing the same. Henceforth the effigies of these men, wearing theMacedonian diadem, appear o
2 CONC n their gold and silver coins. Their patrongods appear on the reverse. This ushurs in the age of royal portraiture.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle of lpsus in Phrygia in 301, called the "battle of the kings",signals the great military clash between Alexander's generals. The warelephant plays an important role in the outcome of this battle and is thesymbol of milit
2 CONC ary strength. The armies of Seleucus and Lysimachus withone hundred and fifty elephants cut off the infantry of Antigonus, leftmortally wounded on the battlefield.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notwithstanding, his son Demetrius rules Phoenicia until 287 when it onceagain passes back to Ptolemy. It remains a dependency of the Ptolemiesfor nearly seventy years. In the year 285 Alexander's empire is neatlydivided between t
2 CONC hree of his former generals, Ptolemy in Egypt, Seleucusin Syria, Lebanon and Palestine, and Lysimachus in Thrace.
2 CONT
2 CONT At his death at the age of eight-four Ptolemy leaves behind him a wellorganized kingdom and the great library at Alexandria. He is succeeded byhis son, Ptolemy 11 Philadelphus (285-246).
2 CONT
2 CONT The persistent lug of war between Ptolemies and Seleucids over Phoenicia,Syria and Palestine also results in great cultural changes in the region.Phoenician is discarded as a literary language and is replaced by Greek.Greek religi
2 CONC ous practices and beliefs take root but at the same time aPhoenician god travels south to Egypt and is honored with great pomp inAlexandria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Byblos is the center for the worship of Adonis, a youth of great beauty,loved by Aphrodite. In legendary tradition, Adonis is hunting the wildboar one day in the company of Aphrodite at Afka, the source of a riverhigh up in the mo
2 CONC untains of Lebanon. The boar turns on him and gores histhigh. Adonis dies of the wound as his blood flows into the river turningthe waters red and the anemones in the river valley scarlet. Aphroditeappeals to Zeus, king of the god
2 CONC s, to bring her lover back to life. Zeuspities the youth and allows him to pass part of the year on earth, theother part underground in Hades. His death is mourned annually at Byblos.He returns in the spring time to the upper worl
2 CONC d and there is greatrejoicing. Adonis in Phoenician means "lord" and is the title given tothe young god of vegetation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theocritus, a Greek poet born in Syracuse c. 315 B.C., lived inAlexandria in the time of Ptolemy if Philadelphus. In his Idyll 15 hedescribes how the Festival of Adonis is celebrated in the city. On thefirst day a great procession
2 CONC  forms as women and children pour out intothe crowded streets to watch. Adonis has come back to life for a briefreunion with Aphrodite and there is great rejoicing. The second day isone of mourning as the women bewail the god's de
2 CONC parture once again forthe underworld.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Alexandria, Adonis is represented by a graceful statue reclining on asilver couch in a temporary bower ornamented with birds and cupids. He isportrayed as a beautiful youth and the women cluster around him as he iscarried throu
2 CONC gh the streets in the procession. The crowd enters the royalpalace as part of the ceremony is performed there. Praises are sung toQueen Berenice, the mother of Philadelphus and Arsinoë, his sister-wife,one way of eulogizing the fa
2 CONC mily of Ptolemy who patronize the festival.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the second day the women lament the departure of the youthful god. Atthe end of the festival the statue of Adonis is carried outside the cityand flung into the sea amidst the wailing and weeping of the women.
2 CONT
2 CONT The years roll by...
2 CONT
2 CONT In Egypt descendants of Ptolemy rule at Alexandria, one after the other.In Syria a line of Seleucid kings, usurpers and imposters alike, sit onthe throne of Antioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Seleucid king Antiochus III the Great (223-187) makes Phoenicia abattlefield in his wars against the Ptolemies. Antiochus III drives theforces of Ptolemy IV Philopator out of Syria, takes Tyre and Acre(Ptolemais) and even thre
2 CONC atens Egypt. In the following years the citiesof Phoenicia pass back and forth between the two powers. In 196 B.C.Phoenicia and Coele Syria (the Bekaa valley) pass into the possession ofthe Seleucid kings. The Phoenician cities we
2 CONC lcome the change, for theestablishment and commercial expansion of Alexandria is a threat to theircommerce.
2 CONT
2 CONT The discovery in 1897 of several painted funerary stelae in a gardensouth of Sidon point to the presence of Greek mercenaries in the armiesof the Seleucids during the second century B.C. These soldiers of fortunefrom the Greek mai
2 CONC nland and cities of Asia Minor died here while onactive duty and were laid to rest forever in foreign land. The stelaetoday are exposed in the Archaeological Museum, Istanbul.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Seleucid monarchy is now in a state of chronic civil war. In thestruggle to seize the throne between the usurper Tryphon and AntiochusVII Sidetes during the latter part of the second century B.C., thesituation becomes so unbea
2 CONC rable that merchants of Beirut desert the cityand open commercial establishments on the Greek island of Delos wherethey conduct a flourishing business.
2 CONT
2 CONT But in the West the rise of Rome presents a danger. The Italian wars of91-83 B.C. keep the Romans at home. The chaotic conditions in Syriapermit Tigranes 11 the Great, king of Armenia, to overrun Cappadocia andexpel one of the las
2 CONC t feeble representatives of the Seleucid monarchy. By83 B.C. Tigranes sits on the throne at Antioch and his frontier extendsto Mount Lebanon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 69 B.C. the Roman general Lucullus arrives in the East, crosses theEuphrates in pursuit of Tigranes and invades Armenia. However his armydoes not support him so he withdraws to Asia Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey replaces Lucullus in 66 B.C. Syria is taken out of the hands ofthe Seleucids once and for all on the ground that they have virtuallyceased to rule. Pompey turns the districts of the Seleucid territory,including Phoenicia, S
2 CONC yria and Palestine into a new province named"Syria". Although this political move consolidates Roman authority in theEast and increases the annual revenue of the Roman treasury, in return ameasure of security is given to the peopl
2 CONC es of the region that they hadnot enjoyed since the conquests of Alexander. Anarchy and piracy isbrought under control and the cities of Phoenicia turn to the sea andtrade.
1 NOTE SOTER; A GENERAL OF ALEXANDER (THE GREAT); (MOTHER PROBABLY A CONCUBINE); HAD
2 CONT SEVERAL ILLEGITIMATE CHILDREN; ABDICATED 285 BC & ESTABLISHED A NEW EGYPTIAN
2 CONT DYNASTY (323 BC-30 BC) LASTING UNTIL THE DEATH OF CLEOPATRA VII (SUCCEEDED BY
2 CONT HIS SON PTOLEMY II PHILADELPHUS)
1 NOTE He was one of the generals of Alexander the Great.
