1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Theoxena of Syracuse=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Theoxena (Greek: Θεόξενα; born before 317 BC; died after 289 BC) was a Greek Macedonian noblewoman. Through her mother’s second marriage, she was a member of the Ptolemaic dynasty and through marriage was a Queen of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Theoxena was the second daughter and third child of the noblewoman Berenice and her first husband Philip. She had two older siblings: a brother called Magas and a sister called Antigone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, Philip, was the son of Amyntas by an unnamed mother. Based on the implying of Plutarch (Pyrrhus 4.4), her father was previously married and had children, including daughters born to him. He served as a military officer
2 CONC  in the service of the Greek King Alexander the Great and was known in commanding one division of the Phalanx in Alexander’s wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother Berenice was a noblewoman from Eordeaea. She was the daughter of local obscure nobleman Magas and noblewoman Antigone. Berenice’s mother was the niece of the powerful Regent Antipater and was a distant collateral relati
2 CONC ve to the Argead dynasty. Her name was either chosen by her parents or she was probably named in honor of a relative either from her mother’s or father’s family.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT About 318 BC, her father died of natural causes. After the death of Theoxena’s father, Theoxena's mother took her and her siblings to Egypt where they were a part of the entourage of her mother’s second maternal cousin Eurydice. E
2 CONC urydice was then the wife of Ptolemy I Soter, the first Greek Pharaoh and founder of the Ptolemaic dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 317 BC, Ptolemy I fell in love with Berenice and divorced Eurydice to marry her. Her mother through her marriage to Ptolemy I, was an Egyptian Queen and the Queen mother of the Ptolemaic dynasty. Through her mother’s marriage t
2 CONC o Ptolemy I, Theoxena was a stepdaughter to Ptolemy I; became an Egyptian Princess living in her stepfather’s court and was a member of the Ptolemaic dynasty. Her mother bore Ptolemy I three children: two daughters, Arsinoe II, Ph
2 CONC ilotera and the future Pharaoh Ptolemy II Philadelphus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around the year 300 BC, Ptolemy I was in engaged in a round of diplomatic marriages involving his children. Theoxena was a part of his strategy by marrying Agathocles of Syracuse, a Syracusan Greek Tyrant of Syracuse and later Kin
2 CONC g of Sicily. Theoxena married Agathocles as his third and final wife. From her marriage to Agathocles, reveals and indicates that Theoxena was a noblewoman of high status, in particular when she lived in Sicily. Little is known on
2 CONC  her relationship with her husband and her life in Sicily. Agathocles became tyrant of Syracuse in 317 BC and declared himself Sicilian King in 305 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a stepmother to Agathocles’ daughter, Lanassa who was the second wife of King Pyrrhus of Epirus. Pyrrhus was previously married to Theoxena’s first sister, Antigone. Theoxena had with Agathocles a son, Archagathus and a da
2 CONC ughter, Theoxena. Her children with Agathocles are only known through surviving archaeological evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Agathocles felt his death was approaching, he sent Theoxena and their children to Egypt. Agathocles died in 289 BC and declared his kingdom a democracy on his death. Theoxena spent her remaining years living in Egypt with her
2 CONC  family. Theoxena and Agathocles’ children and their descendants rosed to very high prominence in the reigns of Theoxena’s half-brother Ptolemy II; her nephew Ptolemy III Euergetes and her great-nephew Ptolemy IV Philopator.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theoxena_of_Syracuse
