1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ptolemy Keraunos (Greek: Πτολεμαῖος Κεραυνός, after 321 BC – 279 BC) was the King of Macedon from 281 BC to 279 BC. His epithet Keraunos is Greek for "Thunder" or "Thunderbolt".
2 CONT
2 CONT Departure from Egypt
2 CONT
2 CONT Ptolemy was the eldest son of Ptolemy I Soter, Pharaoh of Egypt, and his first wife Eurydice, daughter of the regent Antipater.[1] After Keraunos' younger half-brother, also called Ptolemy, was named heir apparent and, in 282 BC, 
2 CONC ascended to the throne as Ptolemy II, he had to leave Egypt, being a potential rival for the throne. He arrived at the court of Lysimachus, the king of Thrace and Macedon, and part of Asia Minor, where his half-sister Arsinoe was 
2 CONC queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Ptolemy was staying in Lysimachus's court, Arsinoe's intrigues led to Lysimachus' first son, Agathocles, being accused of treason and then being executed. Keraunos sided with his other sister (from the same mother) Lysandra,
2 CONC  who was Agathocles' wife, and accompanied her to the court of Seleucus in the East to solicit his aid. Seeing an opportunity to intervene for his own gain in the politics of both Lysimachan Thrace and Ptolemaic Egypt, Seleucus pr
2 CONC epared an expedition against Lysimachus shortly afterwards. King of Macedon
2 CONT
2 CONT After Lysimachus' defeat and death in the Battle of Corupedium in 281 BC against Seleucus, Ptolemy Keraunos murdered Seleucus in order to gain control over the territories of his former protector.[2] He then rushed to Lysimachia w
2 CONC here he had himself acclaimed king by the Macedonian army.[2] At this time he also formally relinquished his claim to the Egyptian throne.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT To secure his position on the throne, Ptolemy asked his half-sister Arsinoe, the widow of Lysimachus, to marry him. Later in the year, he made an alliance with Pyrrhus of Epirus. His only rival, Antigonos Gonatas, son of the ex-ki
2 CONC ng of Macedon, Demetrius I Poliorcetes, was confined in the city of Demetrias, Thessaly, and so Keraunos' power extended to southern Greece as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arsinoe was not happy with the situation. While he was away on a campaign, she conspired against him from the capital, Cassandreia, with the aid of her sons. Keraunos quickly captured Cassandreia, and killed Arsinoe's two younger 
2 CONC sons, while the eldest fled north to the kingdom of the Dardanians. Arsinoe herself fled to Egypt, where she married her own brother, Ptolemy II, and became Arsinoe II.[3] Death Although Ptolemy Keraunos was at the zenith of his p
2 CONC ower, he did not live long afterwards. In 279 BC, he was captured and killed during the wars against the Gauls led by Bolgius who conducted a series of mass raids against Macedon and the rest of Greece.[3] Ptolemy Keraunos' death 
2 CONC brought anarchy to the Greek states, since none of his successors were able to bring stability. This situation lasted about two years, until Antigonos Gonatas defeated the Gauls in a battle near Lysimachia, Thrace, in 277 BC. Afte
2 CONC r this victory he was recognised as king of Macedon and his power extended eventually to southern Greece.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemy_Keraunos
