1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Aeacides of Epirus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Aeacides (Greek: Aἰακίδης; died 313 BC), king of Epirus (331-316, 313 BC), was a son of king Arymbas and grandson of king Alcetas I.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Aeacides married Phthia, the daughter of Menon of Pharsalus, by whom he had the celebrated son Pyrrhus and two daughters, Deidamia and Troias.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded to the throne of Epirus on the death of his cousin Alexander, who was slain in Italy. In 317 BC he assisted Polyperchon in restoring his cousin Olympias and the five-year-old king Alexander IV (mother and son of Alexa
2 CONC nder the Great), to Macedonia. In the following year he marched to the assistance of Olympias, who was hard pressed by Cassander; but the Epirots disliked the service, rose against Aeacides, and drove him from the kingdom. Pyrrhus
2 CONC , who was then only two years old, was with difficulty saved from destruction by some faithful servants. But becoming tired of the Macedonian rule, the Epirots recalled Aeacides in 313 BC; Cassander immediately sent an army agains
2 CONC t him under his brother, Philip, who conquered him the same year in two battles, in the last of which he was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeacides_of_Epirus
