1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Alexander II of Epirus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander II was a king of Epirus, and the son of Pyrrhus and Lanassa, the daughter of the Sicilian tyrant Agathocles.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father as king in 272 BC, and continued the war which his father had begun with Antigonus II Gonatas, whom he succeeded in driving from the kingdom of Macedon. He was, however, dispossessed of both Macedon and Epi
2 CONC rus by Demetrius II of Macedon, the son of Antigonus II; upon which he took refuge amongst the Acarnanians. By their assistance and that of his own subjects, who entertained a great attachment for him, he recovered Epirus. It appe
2 CONC ars that he was in alliance with the Aetolians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander married his paternal half-sister Olympias, by whom he had two sons, Pyrrhus, Ptolemy and a daughter, Phthia. On the death of Alexander, around 242 BC, Olympias assumed the regency on behalf of her sons, and married Phthi
2 CONC a to Demetrius. There are extant silver and copper coins of this king. The former bear a youthful head covered with the skin of an elephant's head. The reverse represents Pallas holding a spear in one hand and a shield in the othe
2 CONC r, and before her stands an eagle on a thunderbolt.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Relations with India'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander is apparently mentioned in the Edicts of Ashoka, as one of the recipients of the Indian Emperor Ashoka's Buddhist proselytism.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_II_of_Epirus
