1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Archelaus (high priest of Comana Cappadocia)=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Archelaus (Greek: Ἀρχέλαος; fl. 1st century BC, died January/February 55 BC) was a high priest of the temple-state of Comana, Cappadocia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family background'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Archelaus was a Cappadocian Greek nobleman, possibly of Macedonian descent. He was the first son and namesake of the Pontic high-ranking General Archelaus who participated in the Mithridatic Wars while his brother was the Pontic s
2 CONC oldier Diogenes, who served in the army of King Mithridates VI of Pontus. His paternal uncle was the distinguished Pontic General Neoptolemus and the family of Archelaus were active in the Pontic Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of his mother is unknown. Archelaus was descended from King Mithridates VI of Pontus. According to Strabo, Archelaus claimed to be a son of Mithridates VI, but this claim is not plausible. Chronologically, Archelaus m
2 CONC ay have been a maternal grandson of the Pontic King; his father, Mithridates VI’s favorite general, may have married one of the daughters of Mithridates VI. There is a possibility that the mother of Archelaus may have been a Ponti
2 CONC c princess, one of the daughters born from the concubine of Mithridates VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a homonymous character. In 63 BC, the Roman Triumvir, General Pompey, appointed Archelaus as High Priest and Roman Client King of the temple state of Comana, Cappadocia.[5] When Pompey raised him to the priesthood, Archelau
2 CONC s became the priest of the Roman Goddess of War, Bellona. The dignity of the priest to the Goddess at Comana conferred upon the person who held it the power of a monarch over the place and its immediate vicinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archelaus had control over the temple serfs, numbering 6,000, and he was not empowered to sell them. A considerable revenue accrued to the priest from the associated territories. By the time of his appointed priesthood and rule, A
2 CONC rchelaus had married an unnamed Greek woman as his first wife and she died by 56 BC. From his first wife, he had two children: one son called Archelaus and an unnamed daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 56 BC the Proconsul of Syria, Aulus Gabinius, was making preparations for the Parthian War and Archelaus travelled to Syria to offer his assistance to Gabinius. As Archelaus planned his trip to Syria, the Greek Ptolemaic Queen 
2 CONC Berenice IV of Egypt wished to marry a prince of royal blood.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Remarriage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 56 BC, Archelaus travelled to Alexandria, Egypt, to offer his hand in marriage to Berenice IV, claiming to be the son of Mithridates VI. Archelaus gave Berenice IV personal gifts and showed his military capabiliti
2 CONC es to woo her into marriage. Some time later, Archelaus and Berenice IV married. It was the second marriage for both. There is a possibility that Berenice IV could have made Archelaus her co-regent and could have given Archelaus t
2 CONC he title of ‘Pharaoh’. A certain papyrus survives dated from their union that could have belonged to Berenice IV and Archelaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''War and death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roman Senate would not allow Archelaus to take part in the Parthian War. Either Archelaus left Gabinius in secret or Gabinius was induced by bribes to assist Archelaus in his hand-in-marriage for Berenice IV, while at the same
2 CONC  time Gabinius received bribes from the father of Berenice IV, Ptolemy XII Auletes, to assist him to be restored to the Egyptian throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archelaus enjoyed the honor of being the husband of Berenice IV for only six months, as Gabinius kept his promise to the father of Berenice IV. In January/February 55 BC, Ptolemy XII Auletes marched on Egypt with an army to reclai
2 CONC m his throne. In the fighting, Archelaus had died. Berenice IV and his daughter from his first marriage were put to death. The Roman Triumvir Mark Antony, who was a friend of his family, had Archelaus’ body searched among the dead
2 CONC  and buried Archelaus in a manner worthy of a King.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son succeeded him as High Priest and Ruler at Comana.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archelaus_(high_priest_of_Comana_Cappadocia)
