2 CONT By G. MASPERO, 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://images.google.co.za/imgres?imgurl=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/17329/17329-h/images/293.jpg&imgrefurl=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/17329/17329-h/v9b.htm&usg=__mmi-hEOeYN0cBs2jkTj_Dz3TXes=&h=207&w=325&sz=19&hl=en&start=4
2 CONC &um=1&itbs=1&tbnid=KR8yuMz8sYxn1M:&tbnh=75&tbnw=118&prev=/images%3Fq%3DArtaxerxes%2BII%2BMemnon%26um%3D1%26hl%3Den%26sa%3DN%26tbs%3Disch:1
2 CONT
2 CONT On ascending the throne, Ochus assumed the name of Darius. His confidential advisers were three eunuchs, who ruled the empire in his name—Artoxares, who had taken such a prominent part in the campaign which won him the crown, Arti
2 CONC barzanes, and Athôos; but the guiding spirit of his government was, in reality, his wife, the detestable Parysatis. She had already borne him two children before she became queen; a daughter, Amestris, and a son, Arsaces, who afte
2 CONC rwards became king under the name of Artaxerxes. Soon after the accession of her husband, she bore him a second son, whom she named Cyrus, in memory of the founder of the empire, and a daughter, Artostê; several other children wer
2 CONC e born subsequently, making thirteen in all, but these all died in childhood, except one named Oxendras. Violent, false, jealous, and passionately fond of the exercise of power, Parysatis hesitated at no crime to rid herself of th
2 CONC ose who thwarted her schemes, even though they might be members of her own family; and, not content with putting them out of the way, she delighted in making them taste her hatred to the full, by subjecting them to the most skilfu
2 CONC lly graduated refinements of torture; she deservedly left behind her the reputation of being one of the most cruel of all the cruel queens, whose memory was a terror not only to the harems of Persia, but to the whole of the Easter
2 CONC n world. The numerous revolts which broke out soon after her husband's accession, furnished occasions for the revelation of her perfidious cleverness....
2 CONT
2 CONT Cyrus (artaxerxes' brother) entered the temple of Pasargadae surreptitiously during the coronation ceremony, with the intention of killing his brother at the foot of the altar; but Tissaphernes, warned by one of the priests, denou
2 CONC nced him, and he would have been put to death on the spot, had not his mother thrown her arms around him and prevented the executioner from fulfilling his office. Having with difficulty obtained pardon and been sent back to his pr
2 CONC ovince, he collected thirty thousand Greeks and a hundred thousand native troops, and, hastily leaving Sardes (401 B.C.), he crossed Asia Minor, Northern Syria, and Mesopotamia, encountered the royal army at Cunaxa, to the north o
2 CONC f Babylon, and rashly met his end at the very moment of victory. He was a brave, active, and generous prince, endowed with all the virtues requisite to make a good Oriental monarch, and he had, moreover, learnt, through contact wi
2 CONC th the Greeks, to recognise the weak points of his own nation, and was fully determined to remedy them: his death, perhaps, was an irreparable misfortune for his country. Had he survived and supplanted the feeble Artaxerxes, it is
2 CONC  quite possible that he might have confirmed and strengthened the power of Persia, or, at least, temporarily have arrested its decline.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Artaxerxes II Mnemon, King of Persia, was born circa 456 BC; died circa 358 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Artaxerxes II Mnemon (Old Persian: 𐎠𐎼𐎫𐎧𐏁𐏂𐎠[1] Artaxšaçā, Ancient Greek: Ἀρταξέρξης) was king of Persia from 404 BC until his death. He was a son of Darius II of Persia and Parysatis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He defended his position against his brother Cyrus the Younger, who was defeated and killed at the Battle of Cunaxa in 401 BC, and against a revolt of the provincial governors, the satraps (366 – 358 BC). He also became involved i
2 CONC n a war with Persia's erstwhile allies, the Spartans, who, under Agesilaus II, invaded Asia Minor. In order to redirect the Spartans attention to Greek affairs, Artaxerxes subsidized their enemies: in particular the Athenians, The
2 CONC bans, and Corinthians. These subsidies helped to engage the Spartans in what would become known as the Corinthian War. In 386 BC, Artaxerxes II betrayed his allies and came to an arrangement with Sparta, and in the Treaty of Antal
2 CONC cidas he forced his erstwhile allies to come to terms. This treaty restored control of the Greek cities of Ionia and Aeolis on the Anatolian coast to the Persians, while giving Sparta dominance on the Greek mainland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although successful against the Greeks, Artaxerxes had more trouble with the Egyptians, who had successfully revolted against him at the beginning of his reign. An attempt to reconquer Egypt in 373 BC was completely unsuccessful, 
2 CONC but in his waning years the Persians did manage to defeat a joint Egyptian–Spartan effort to conquer Phoenicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is reported to have had a number of wives. His main wife was Stateira, until she was poisoned by Artaxerxes' mother Parysatis in about 400 BC. Another chief wife was a Greek woman of Phocaea named Aspasia (not the same as the c
2 CONC oncubine of Pericles). Artaxerxes II is said to have more than 115 sons from 350 wives.[2] He also is said to have loved a young eunuch by the name of Tiridates, who died "as he was emerging from childhood". His death caused Artax
2 CONC erxes enormous grief, and there was public mourning for him throughout the empire as an offering to the king from his subjects. According to Claudius Aelianus, Artaxerxes was brought out of the mourning by Aspasia, after she wore 
2 CONC the eunuch's cloak over her dress.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is identified as the Persian king Ahasuerus of the Purim story in traditional sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much of Artaxerxes's wealth was spent on building projects. He restored the palace of Darius I at Susa,[4] and also the fortifications; including a strong redoubt at the southeast corner of the enclosure and gave Ecbatana a new ap
2 CONC adana and sculptures. He seems not to have built much at Persepolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Artaxerxes II Memnon (Old Persian: Artaxšaçrā, Persian: اردشیر - Ardašir, Ancient Greek: Αρταξέρξης) (ca. 436 – 358 BC) was king of Persia from 404 BC until his death. He defended his position against his brother Cyrus the Younger
2 CONC , who was defeated and killed at the Battle of Cunaxa in 401 BC, and against a revolt of the provincial governors, the satraps (366 – 358 BC). He also became involved in a war with Persia's erstwhile allies, the Spartans, who, und
2 CONC er Agesilaus, invaded Asia Minor. To keep the Spartans busy, Artaxerxes subsidized their enemies in Greece—the Athenians, Thebans, and Corinthians, especially—to keep them busy back at home, in what would become known as the Corin
2 CONC thian War. In 386 BC, Artaxerxes II betrayed his allies and came to an arrangement with Sparta, and in the Treaty of Antalcidas he forced his erstwhile allies to come to terms. This treaty restored control of the Greek cities of I
2 CONC onia and Aeolis on the Anatolian coast to the Persians, while giving Sparta dominance on the Greek mainland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although thus rather successful against the Greeks, Artaxerxes had more trouble with the Egyptians, who had successfully revolted against him at the beginning of his reign. An attempt to reconquer Egypt in 373 BC was completely un
2 CONC successful, but in his waning years the Persians did manage to defeat a joint Egyptian–Spartan effort to conquer Phoenicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is reported to have had a number of wives, chief among whom was a Greek woman of Phocaea named Aspasia (not the same as the concubine of Pericles). He also is said to have loved a young eunuch by the name of Tiridates, who died
2 CONC  "as he was emerging from childhood". His death caused Artaxerxes enormous grief, and there was public mourning for him throughout the empire as an offering to the king from his subjects.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is thought to be one of the prime candidates for the Persian king Ahasuerus of the Purim story.
2 CONT
