1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Antander=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Antander (Greek: Ἄντανδρος - or Andro as he is called by the historian Orosius) was a man of Syracuse of the 3rd and 4th centuries BC. He was the older brother of Agathocles, king of Syracuse, and was a commander -- or strategos -
2 CONC - of the troops sent by the Syracusans to the relief of Crotona when it was besieged by the Bruttii tribe in 317 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siege of Syracuse'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 4th century BCE, Sicily was invaded by Carthage and Syracuse itself was besieged by the Carthaginians. In 310 BCE, Agathocles hatched a daring plan to sneak out of Sicily with a sizable force to invade Libya, to draw t
2 CONC he Carthaginians out of Sicily, leaving Antander together with Erymnon in command of Syracuse. During the absence of Agathocles in Africa, the Carthaginians under Hamilcar pressed their siege.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some ancient sources report that Antander wished to surrender to Hamilcar after receiving news that Agathocles's fleet had been burned, and that Erymnon persuaded him not to. More recent scholarship indicates that Hamilcar tried a
2 CONC  ruse to convince Antander his brother's army had been destroyed by showing Antander the charred remains of his brother's boats, while in reality Agathocles had burned his own ships as a tactic to move to a land assault. A debate 
2 CONC erupted in the Syracusan assembly in which a man named Diognetus (or Phalaeneus), proposed surrendering to the Carthaginians, and Antander had the man arrested to prevent the spread of the idea to the besieged Syracusans. In any c
2 CONC ase, Syracuse survived the siege.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Executions'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Antander is mentioned afterwards as the instrument of his brother's cruelty: in 307 BC, Agathocles commanded Antander to execute the families of all the rebellious Syracusan soldiers who remained in Africa (whom Agathocles blamed 
2 CONC for the death of his sons), and Antander is said to have ruthlessly carried out this order, slaughtering men, women, children, and the elderly. Even more horrifying to the ancient mind, Antander's forces piled these bodies on the 
2 CONC beach for the waves to take, denying them burial rites.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Agathocles's absence from Syracuse, silver tetradrachms (previously issued under Agathocles's name only) were issued with Antander's monogram. Antander was also the author of a historical work - an apologetic biography of h
2 CONC is brother Agathocles - which is quoted by the ancient historian Diodorus Siculus.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known how long Antander lived, only that he outlived his brother Agathocles, who died in 289 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antander
