1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Marsyas of Pella==
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsyas_of_Pella En]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marsyas of Pella''' (Ancient Greek: Μαρσύας Περιάνδρου Πελλαῖος; c. 356 BC – c. 294 BC [citation needed]), son of Periander, was a Greek historian. According to the Suda Encyclopedia, he was a brother of [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/Antigonus-the-One-eyed/6000000003645908452 '''Antigonus I Monophthalmus'''], who was afterwards king of Asia, by which an uterine brother alone can be meant, as the father of Antigonus was named [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Phillip/6000000024434932199 '''Philip''']. Both of these statements point to his being of noble birth, and appear strangely at variance with the assertion that he was a mere professional grammarian Grammatodidascalus, a statement 
2 CONC which Robert Geier[1] conjectures plausibly enough to refer in fact to Marsyas of Philippi. Suidas, indeed, seems in many points to have confounded the two. The only other fact transmitted to us concerning the life of Marsyas, is 
2 CONC that he was appointed by [https://www.geni.com/people/Demetrius-Poliorcetes-king-of-Macedonia/6000000007265083885 '''Demetrius Poliorcetes'''] to command one division of his fleet in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Sa
2 CONC lamis_(306_BC)_ Battle of Salamis in Cyprus (306 BC)] ([https://www.geni.com/people/Diodorus-of-Sicily/6000000078667679922 '''Diodorus'''], xx. 50.). However, this circumstance is alone sufficient to show that he was a person who 
2 CONC himself took an active part in public affairs, not a mere man of letters. It is probable that he followed the fortunes of his stepbrother [https://www.geni.com/people/Antigonus-the-One-eyed/6000000003645908452 '''Antigonus'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT His principal work was a history of Macedonia, Makedonika, in 10 books, commencing from the earliest times, and coming down to the wars of Alexander in Asia, when it terminated abruptly in 331 BC, with the return of the monarch in
2 CONC to Syria, after the conquest of Egypt and the foundation of Alexandria. It is repeatedly cited by Athenaeus, Plutarch, Harpocration, Gnaeus Pompeius Trogus and Justin (historian). Suidas also speaks of a history on the education o
2 CONC f Alexander, (Αλεξάνδρου αγωγή) and a treatise on the history of antiquities of Athens (Αττικά) in 12 books, which is considered by Bernhardy and Geier to be the same with Archaeology of Marsyas the younger. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsyas_of_Pella#References References]====
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsyas_of_Pella#External_links External links]====
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0104%3Aalphabetic+letter%3DM%3Aentry+group%3D11%3Aentry%3Dmarsyas-bio-4 A Dictionary of Greek and Roman biography and mythology, Ma, Ma'rius, Ma'rsyas]
2 CONT * [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1467-3217 Marsyas of Pella and Phrygia] by author Mrs. Sabine Müller, Philipps-University Marburg, ELECTRUM Vol. 29 (2022): 39 - 51 doi:10.4467/20800909EL.22.004.15774 [https://ejournals.eu/en/journa
2 CONC l/electrum Electrum Journal of Ancient History]
