1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Stratonice of Libya=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Stratonice (Greek: Στρατονίκη; fl. possibly late second half of 4th century BC and first half of 3rd century BC) was a Greek noblewoman of very high status and was the wife of the Ptolemaic official Archagathus of Libya.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stratonice is an unattested person and is only known through surviving evidence. When her husband was an Epistates in Libya serving in Cyrenaica, Stratonice and her husband Archagathus on marble piece made a dedication of a temeno
2 CONC s to Isis and Serapis at Alexandria on behalf of Archagathus’ maternal uncle, the Ptolemaic prince Ptolemy II Philadelphus and his maternal grandmother the first Ptolemaic Queen Berenice I of Egypt. The record is dated from ca. 28
2 CONC 3-278 BC and is on display in the Greco-Roman Museum of Alexandria. The inscription below translated in Greek and English reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''ὑπὲρ βασιλέως Πτολεμαίου
2 CONT τοῦ Πτολεμαίου καὶ Βερενίκης Σωτήρων Άρχάγαθος Άγαθοκλέους ὁ ἐπιστάτης τῆς Λιβύης καὶ ἡ γυνὴ Στρατονίκη Σαράπιδι Ἴσιδι τὸ τέμενος. King Ptolemy son of Ptolemy and Berenice the Saviours Archagathus son of Agathocles epistates of Li
2 CONC bya and his wife Stratonice Serapis, Isis of temenos.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Stratonice made a dedication, to Ptolemy II’s sister the Ptolemaic princess Arsinoe II. The original statue she dedicated was a Hellenistic original statue of Arsinoe II which must be dated from 280 BC-278 BC. The original Helleni
2 CONC stic statue of Arsinoe II was later copied as a Roman statue. The text from the Roman copy below translated in Greek and English reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Βασίλισσαν Ὰρσινόην βασιλέως Πτολεμαίου καί βασιλίσσης Βερενίκης Στρατονίκη βασιλέως Δημητρίου Queen Arsinoe daughter of King Ptolemy and Queen Berenice Stratonice daughter of King Demetrius''
2 CONT
2 CONT The inscription reveals she was a daughter of a King Demetrius and there is a possibility that she could identified as an otherwise-unknown daughter of Greek Macedonian King Demetrius I Poliorcetes.[9] According to the suggestions
2 CONC  of L. Morretti (RFIC 93, 1965, 73) and R.S. Bagnall (Philologus 120, 1976, 195) that Stratonice may have been the sister of Alexander, son of Demetrius I Poliorcetes and Deidamia. Alexander lived his remaining life as an honorary
2 CONC  hostage in Egypt and Stratonice may have accompanied him to Egypt. Alexander and Stratonice may have accompanied their maternal uncle Pyrrhus of Epirus to Egypt in c.299 BC when Pyrrhus lived as a hostage in the country.[11] Stra
2 CONC tonice is not to be confused with Stratonice of Syria, the known daughter of Demetrius I Poliorcetes of this name, who married as the second wife of Seleucus I Nicator and the wife of Antiochus I Soter. If this suggestion is corre
2 CONC ct, Stratonice was born at the same time as her husband between 301 BC-298 BC. There is no record of any children born to her marriage to Archagathus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stratonice may have been the same Stratonice who had a celebrated Mausoleum at Eleusis near Alexandria and was a later mistress of Ptolemy II.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stratonice_of_Libya
