2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Glaphyra=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Glaphyra (Greek: Γλαφύρα) was a Hetaera, a form of courtesan, who lived in the 1st century BC. Glaphyra was famed and celebrated in antiquity for her beauty, charm and seductiveness. Her marriage to Archelaus the elder of Cappadoc
2 CONC ia gave her political power. Her later affair with Mark Antony occasioned a vulgar poem from Octavian Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage to Archelaus'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Glaphyra was a Greek woman from Cappadocia from obscure origins. Glaphyra had married a Cappadocian Greek nobleman called Archelaus, the High Priest Ruler of the temple state of Comana, Cappadocia. Archelaus was the High Priest of
2 CONC  the Roman Goddess of War, Bellona. Through her marriage to Archelaus, Glaphyra became a ruler of the temple state. Archelaus' father of the same name had descended from King Mithridates VI of Pontus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Glaphyra bore Archelaus two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Archelaus Sisines,[6] also known as King Archelaus of Cappadocia who reigned from 36 BC until his death in 17 AD
2 CONT # Sisines
2 CONT
2 CONT In 47 BC the Roman Dictator Gaius Julius Caesar after the conclusion of his military victory against the Triumvir Pompey, deprived and deposed Archelaus of his office of high priest and rule over Comana. Archelaus was replaced by 
2 CONC another Greek nobleman called Lycomedes. Pompey was their family patron and it was he who appointed his father as High Priest Ruler of the temple state of Comana. Sometime later Archelaus died at an unknown date. After the death o
2 CONC f Archelaus, Glaphyra remained in Cappadocia with her sons. Glaphyra could be seen as the widow of the dynast of Comana.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Affair with Antony'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Years later, Glaphyra became one of the mistresses to the Roman Triumvir Mark Antony. Through her beauty, Glaphyra had influenced and induced Antony to designate and install her first son as King of Cappadocia. In 36 BC, Antony re
2 CONC moved and executed Ariarathes X of Cappadocia from his throne and installed Archelaus as the successor of Ariarathes X.
2 CONT
2 CONT Glaphyra appeared to be a powerful lady at the Royal Court and was involved internal politics in Cappadocia.[16] Her powerful influence can be demonstrated by contemporary invective about the time of the Perusine War in 41 BC, by 
2 CONC certain frank and famous verses which Triumvir Octavian composed about Antony.[17] Antony had fallen in love with her. Octavian wrote a vulgar squib about the affair, referring to Antony's wife Fulvia:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''"Because Antony fucks Glaphyra, Fulvia has arranged
2 CONT this punishment for me: that I fuck her too. That I fuck Fulvia? What if Manius begged me to bugger him? Would I? I don't think so, if I were sane. 'Either fuck or let's fight,' she says. Doesn't she know my prick is dearer to me 
2 CONC than life itself? Let the trumpets blare!"''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaphyra_(hetaera)
