1 NOTE OR "DARIUS II OCHUS/OKHOS (THE BASTARD); GREAT KING OF PERSIA 423-404 BC;
2 CONT PER-'O AS MERY.AMEN.RE' 423-404 BC; b. 475 BC-d. 404 BC; HAD 13 OTHER CHILDREN
2 CONT BESIDES THOSE SHOWN
1 NOTE He ruled from 424 to 404 b.c.
1 NOTE He ruled from 424 to 404 b.c.
1 NOTE His original was name Ochus. He was an illegitimate son hence his Greek 
2 CONT name, Nothos, meaning "bastard." As Ochus, he was a satrap of the province 
2 CONT of Hyrcania when his father died. Ochus's half brother, Xerxes II, was 
2 CONT king for a few weeks; another half brother, Sogdianus, murdered Xerxes II 
2 CONT and was king for a few months; then Ochus murdered Sogdianus and ascended 
2 CONT the throne, assuming the name of Darius. In the early years of his reign 
2 CONT the power of Athens prevented him from interfering in the affairs of 
2 CONT Greece. But after Athens was defeated by Syracuse in 413 bc, Darius II 
2 CONT intervened and supported Sparta for the remaining years of the 
2 CONT Peloponnesian War. In 407 bc he sent his son Cyrus the Younger to command 
2 CONT the combined Persian and Spartan forces in Asia Minor. Three years later 
2 CONT he died. The 20 years of Darius's reign were notable primarily for 
2 CONT ruthless suppression of a series of revolts within his empire.
