1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Hicetas, tyrant of Syracuse (289 BCE - 280 BCE)==
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hicetas_(tyrant_of_Syracuse)_ En]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hicetas''' (Greek: Ἱκέτας or Ἱκέτης) was tyrant of Syracuse, Magna Graecia, during the interval between the reign of [https://www.geni.com/people/Agathocles-tyrant-of-Syracuse/6000000003645882963 '''Agathocles'''] and that of [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/people/Pyrrhus-I-king-of-Epirus/6000000002187720008 '''Pyrrhus'''].[1] After the death of [https://www.geni.com/people/Agathocles-tyrant-of-Syracuse/6000000003645882963 '''Agathocles'''] (289 BC) his supposed 
2 CONC assassin, Maenon, put to death [https://www.geni.com/people/Archagathus/6000000041106717514 '''Archagathus'''], the grandson of [https://www.geni.com/people/Agathocles-tyrant-of-Syracuse/6000000003645882963 '''Agathocles''']; and,
2 CONC  assuming the command of the army with which the latter was besieging [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aetna_(city)_ Aetna], directed his arms against Syracuse. Hereupon Hicetas was sent against him by the Syracusans, with a conside
2 CONC rable army: but after the war had continued for some time, without any decisive result, Maenon, by calling in the aid of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carthage Carthaginians], obtained the superiority, and the Syracusans were
2 CONC  compelled to conclude an ignominious peace.[1] Soon after ensued the revolution which led to the expulsion of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campania Campanian] mercenaries, afterwards known as the [https://en.wikipedia.org/w
2 CONC iki/Mamertines Mamertines]; and it must have been shortly after this that Hicetas established himself in the supreme power, as [https://www.geni.com/people/Diodorus-of-Sicily/6000000078667679922 '''Diodorus Siculus'''] relates[2] 
2 CONC that he ruled for nine years. The only events of his government that are recorded are a war with [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phintias_of_Agrigentum Phintias], tyrant of Agrigentum (modern [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrigent
2 CONC o Agrigento]), in which he obtained a considerable victory, and one with the Carthaginians, by whom he was defeated at the river Terias.[1] He was at length expelled from Syracuse by Thynion, an event which took place not long bef
2 CONC ore the arrival of [https://www.geni.com/people/Pyrrhus-I-king-of-Epirus/6000000002187720008 '''Pyrrhus'''] in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sicily Sicily], and must therefore be referred either to 279 BC or 278 BC, either of whi
2 CONC ch dates is consistent enough with the period of nine years allotted to his reign by Diodorus.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT There are extant gold coins struck at Syracuse bearing the name of Hicetas: from the inscription on these ΕΠΙ ΙΚΕΤΑ, it is clear that Hicetas — unlike his contemporary [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phintias_of_Agrigentum Phintias
2 CONC  of Agrigentum] — never assumed the title of "king."[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hicetas_(tyrant_of_Syracuse)#References References]====
