2 TITL Roxana wife of Alexander the Great (47GGM)
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE Roxane (Bactrian Roshanak): Bactrian princess (c.343-310, official wifeof Alexander the Great.
2 CONT Roxane -her name Roshanak means 'little star'- was the daughter of aSogdian nobleman named Oxyartes (Vaxšuvadarva), who defended a mountainfortress against the invading army of the Macedonian king Alexander theGreat (327). When th
2 CONC e fortress was captured, the sixteen year old woman,was among the prisoners of war. (Click here for the story.)
2 CONT
2 CONT At that stage of his campaign in the Achaemenid empire, it had becomeobvious that Alexander could not control his conquests without doingconcessions to the native population. He had to show respect to theircustoms if he wanted the
2 CONC ir respect. In the years 330-327, we seeAlexander appointing Persians in important functions, dress himself likean Iranian nobleman, introduce the oriental court ritual (proskynesis).Marrying a local princess was a logical step.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sodoma, Wedding of
2 CONT Alexander and Roxane
2 CONT (1514)
2 CONT The obvious candidate was Barsine, who had been Alexander's concubinesince the battle of Issus in November 333. But this woman, who hadalready given birth to a son, was ignored. It is not known why, but shemay have been unacceptab
2 CONC le to the Iranian population, because she hadspend a great deal of her life in Europe. Barsine may have been tooGreek; an extra argument for this hypothesis is that later, she settledherself in Greece. Another explanation may be t
2 CONC hat Alexander had simplyfallen in love with Roxane.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage was concluded according to the local customs (click here fora description), and Roxane followed her husband when he invaded India(326) and returned to Babylonia (325-324). On June 11, 323, her husbanddied. She was pre
2 CONC gnant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the next years, the era of the Diadochi, she had a very difficultposition. One of the commanders, Perdiccas, was chosen as regent forAlexander's mentally deficient brother Philip Arridaeus and hisposthumous son, Alexander. Toge
2 CONC ther, Perdiccas and Roxane executedAlexander's second wife, Statira, who could become a rival. However,Perdiccas died soon after, and the Macedonian commanders came together todiscuss the future. During this meeting at Triparadisu
2 CONC s, they decided todivide the empire (click here for the story). A new but weak regent, theold Antipater, was appointed. He ordered Roxane, Philip and the baby togo to Macedonia. For a woman who had grown up in Sogdiana, had seenIn
2 CONC dia, had lived in the palaces of Persia and Babylonia, it must havebeen a strange experience to live in barbarous Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT For several years, she and her son were safe, but one of the rivalcommanders, Cassander, captured them in 316. Five years later, Cassanderand the other Diadochi concluded a peace treaty in which they agreed thatthey would remain i
2 CONC n charge of the empire until Roxane's son would becomeking (in 305). This was of course an incentive to execute the boy kingand his mother, who were executed immediately (311/310).
2 CONT
2 CONT The end of the Macedonian royal house
2 CONT
2 CONT  In 311, the Diadochi concluded a peace treaty. Ptolemy and Lysimachuswere confirmed in their territories, Egypt and Thrace; Cassander andAntigonus remained supreme commanders of the Macedonian forces in Europeand Asia; the Greek 
2 CONC towns were recognized by all parties as 'free andautonomous' (but Cassander kept garrisons at several places); and it wasagreed that the boy king Alexander, son of Alexander the Great andRoxane, would become sole ruler of the enti
2 CONC re empire when he came of age,in 305. The result was, of course, that the royals were killed as soon aspossible. Diodorus of Sicily tells the story in his World history(19.105.1-4); the translation was made by M.M. Austin..
2 CONT
2 CONT In the archonship of Simonides at Athens [1], Cassander, Ptolemy andLysimachus put an end to the war against Antigonus and concluded atreaty. It was specified in it that Cassander should be general of Europeuntil Alexander, Roxane
2 CONC 's son, should come of age, that Lysimachus shouldbe master of Thrace and Ptolemy master of Egypt and the neighboringcities in Libya and Arabia, that Antigonus should command the whole ofAsia, while the Greeks should be autonomous
2 CONC .
2 CONT Nevertheless they failed to abide by this agreement, and each of them putforward fair pretexts and sought to increase his power. Cassander sawthat Roxane's son Alexander was growing up and that there were some whowere spreading th
2 CONC e word in Macedonia that one ought to release the boyfrom custody and hand over to him his father's kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Afraid for his own safety, he instructed Glaucias, who was in charge ofthe boy's custody, to assassinate Roxane and the king and conceal theirbodies, and not to report the deed to any of the others. Glaucias carriedout the orders,
2 CONC  and this freed Cassander, Lysimachus, Ptolemy and evenAntigonus from anticipated fears about the king. For now that there wasno one to take over the empire, those who ruled peoples or cities couldeach entertain hopes of kingship 
2 CONC and controlled henceforward theterritory under their power like kingdoms that had been conquered in war.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Note 1:
2 CONT He gave his name to the year 311/310.
