1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Admetus''' (Greek: Αδμητος) (before 470-430 BC) was a Greek king of the Molossians at the time that [http://www.geni.com/people/Themistocles/6000000017270762741 Themistocles] (524–459 BCE) was the effective rule
2 CONC r of Athens. His ancestry is mythological. He is said to have been a son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Alcon-of-Epirus/6000000002837440340 Alcon of Epirus] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Agariste-of-Sicyon/6000000002837440346 Ag
2 CONC ariste of Sicyon].
2 CONT
2 CONT See Wikipedia, [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molossians Molossians]; John Lemprière, [http://books.google.com/books?id=xeE_AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA537&lpg=PA537&dq=admetus+of+epirus&source=bl&ots=LCZPNgm7yr&sig=HoJziuPUymKp7Tmj0HTdumX644A&h
2 CONC l=en&sa=X&ei=te8bUKyLNqOy2wW_7oAo&ved=0CGsQ6AEwBg#v=onepage&q=admetus%20of%20epirus&f=false Bibliotheca Classica] (1831), 537.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Wikipedia Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Admetus_of_Epirus
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Admetus''' (Greek: Αδμητος) (before 470-430 BC) was a Greek king of the Molossians at the time that Themistocles (524–459 BC) was the effective ruler of Athens. When Themistocles was in control of Athens, Admetus had opposed hi
2 CONC m, but without any rancour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Themistocles, when fleeing from the Athenian officers who were ordered to seize him when he had been accused of being a party to the treason of Pausanias, found himself unable to stay in Corcyra. So Themistocles travelled to
2 CONC  Epirus and found his only hope of refuge was the house of Admetus. As Admetus was absent, Admetus' queen, Phthia, welcomed Themistocles. On his return to Epirus, Admetus assured Themistocles of his protection.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Plutarch, Admetus ignored everything that the Athenian and Lacedaemonian commissioners, who arrived at Epirus soon afterwards, could say; and later Admetus arranged for Themistocles to be safely sent to Pydna on his w
2 CONC ay to the Persian court.[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT === References ===
2 CONT
2 CONT # ^ Thucydides, i. 136, 137.
2 CONT # ^ Plutarch. Themistocles, 24.
2 CONT # ^ Clough, Arthur Hugh (1867), "Admetus (2)", in Smith, William, Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, 1, Boston, pp. 19 [edit]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Admetus of Epirus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Admetus (Greek: Αδμητος) (before 470-430 BC) was a Greek king of the Molossians at the time that Themistocles (524-459 BC) was the effective ruler of Athens. When Themistocles was in control of Athens, Admetus had opposed him, but
2 CONC  without any rancour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Themistocles, when fleeing from the Athenian officers who were ordered to seize him when he had been accused of being a party to the treason of Pausanias, found himself unable to stay in Corcyra. So Themistocles travelled to
2 CONC  Epirus and found his only hope of refuge was the house of Admetus. As Admetus was absent, Admetus' queen, Phthia, welcomed Themistocles. On his return to Epirus, Admetus assured Themistocles of his protection.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Plutarch, Admetus ignored everything that the Athenian and Lacedaemonian commissioners, who arrived at Epirus soon afterwards, could say; and later Admetus arranged for Themistocles to be safely sent to Pydna on his w
2 CONC ay to the Persian court.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
