1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Drusilla (38–79 C.E.)''' was the youngest daughter of King Agrippa I. Her first betrothal, arranged by her father, to Epiphanes, prince of Commagene fell through in 44 C.E., with her father’s death, when she was
2 CONC  only six years old (Ant. 18:355). Josephus further relates that she was later married to another foreign royalty, Aziz, king of Emesa , only to divorce him when courted by the Roman governor of Judaea, Felix, the Emperor Claudius
2 CONC ’ freedman (Ant. 20:139, 141). She is usually mentioned in the literature in order to draw attention to the fact that all of Agrippa’s daughters initiated their own divorce. One interesting detail which Josephus adds is that she a
2 CONC nd her son Felix both died at Pompeii, in the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 C.E. (Ant. 20:144). http://jwa.org/encyclopedia/article/herodian-women
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Drusilla (born 38, died August 25, AD 79) was a daughter of Herod Agrippa I and thus sister to Berenice, Mariamne and Herod Agrippa II.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===First marriage===
2 CONT She was six years of age at the time of her father's death at Caesarea in 44. Her father had betrothed her to Gaius Julius Archelaus Antiochus Epiphanes, first son of King Antiochus IV of Commagene,[1] with a stipulation from her 
2 CONC father that Epiphanes should embrace the Jewish religion.[2] The prince in the end refused to abide by his promise to do so, and the marriage had still not been contracted on her father's death. On Agrippa's death:
2 CONT
2 CONT >“	...the inhabitants of Caesarea and of Sebaste forgot the kindnesses he had bestowed on them, and acted the part of the bitterest enemies; for they cast such reproaches upon the deceased as are not fit to be spoken of; and so ma
2 CONC ny of them as were then soldiers, which were a great number, went to his house, and hastily carried off the statues of [Agrippa I]'s daughters, and all at once carried them into the brothels, and when they had set them on the brot
2 CONC hel roofs, they abused them to the utmost of their power, and did such things to them as are too indecent to be related. They also laid themselves down in public places, and celebrated general feastings, with garlands on their hea
2 CONC ds, and with ointments and libations to Charon, and drinking to one another for joy that the king was expired, not only unmindful of Agrippa, who had extended his liberality to them in abundance, but also of his grandfather Herod 
2 CONC the Great, who had himself rebuilt their cities, and had raised them havens and temples at vast expense.[3]	”
2 CONT
2 CONT Once Drusilla's brother, Herod Agrippa II, had been assigned the tetrachy of Herod Philip I (along with Batanea, Trachonites and Abila) in around 49/50, he broke off her engagement to Epiphanes and gave her in marriage to Gaius Ju
2 CONC lius Azizus, Priest King of Emesa, who, in order to obtain her hand, consented to be circumcised.[2] Herod also at around this time married Mariamne to her betrothed, Gaius the prince of Commagene. Please see & contribute to  [htt
2 CONC p://www.geni.com/discussions/108190?msg=788136 Discussion] on this betrothal.
2 CONT ===Felix===
2 CONT It appears that it was shortly after her first marriage was contracted that Antonius Felix, the Roman procurator of Judaea, met Drusilla, probably at her brother's court (Berenice, the elder sister, lived with her brother at this 
2 CONC time, and thus Drusilla probably did too). Felix was struck by the great beauty of Drusilla, and determined to make her his (second) wife. In order to persuade her, a practising Jew, to divorce her Jewish husband and marry him, a 
2 CONC pagan, he took the following steps:
2 CONT >“	While Felix was procurator of Judea, he saw this Drusilla, and fell in love with her; for she did indeed exceed all other women in beauty; and he sent to her a person whose name was Simon[4] (Note: in some manuscripts, Atomos),
2 CONC  a Jewish friend of his, by birth a Cypriot, who pretended to be a magician. Simon endeavored to persuade her to forsake her present husband, and marry Felix; and promised, that if she would not refuse Felix, he would make her a h
2 CONC appy woman. Accordingly she acted unwisely and, because she longed to avoid her sister Berenice's envy (for Drusilla was very ill-treated by Berenice because of Drusilla's beauty) was prevailed upon to transgress the laws of her f
2 CONC orefathers, and to marry Felix.[5]	”
2 CONT
2 CONT She was about twenty-two years of age when she appeared at Felix's side, during St. Paul's captivity at Caesarea - Acts 24:24 reports her thus:
2 CONT "Several days later Felix came [back into court] with his wife Drusilla, who was a Jewess." Acts gives no further information on her subsequent life, though Josephus states that they had a son named Marcus Antonius Agrippa and a d
2 CONC aughter Antonia Clementiana. Their son perished together with his mother Drusilla, along with noted Roman historian Pliny the Elder plus most of the populations of Pompeii and Herculaneum in the AD 79 eruption of Mount Vesuvius.[6
2 CONC ] For further information about the family of Felix and Drusilla, see Antonius Felix.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT #^ Josephus, Jewish Antiquities, xix. 9. § 1.
2 CONT #^ a b Josephus, Jewish Antiquities, xx.7.1
2 CONT #^ Josephus, Jewish Antiquities, xix. 9. 1 and xx.7.1
2 CONT #^ Atomos in some manuscripts
2 CONT #^ Josephus, Jewish Antiquities, xx.7.2
2 CONT #^ Mentioned in Josephus, Jewish Antiquities, xx.7.2, and in a lost section of the work.http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drusilla_(daughter_of_Herod_Agrippa_I)
2 CONT
2 CONT >>______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Wagner, Sir Anthony Richard; ‘Pedigree and Progress: Essays in the Genealogical Interpretation of History’
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Drusila de Judea derivado del celta Drausus. Hija del rey Herodes Agripa I y esposa del procurador romano Félix (Hch. 24:24). Nació en el 38 d.C. y sólo tenía 6 años cuando murió su padre. A una edad temprana se comprometió con Ep
2 CONC ífanes, el hijo del rey Antíoco de Comagene, pero Epífanes más tarde rehusó casarse con ella, porque esto hubiera requerido que se hiciera judío y aceptara la circuncisión. Entonces ella se casó con el rey Azizus (?) de Emesa, a q
2 CONC uien abandonó poco después para casarse con Félix. Tuvo un hijo con Félix.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leer más: Diccionario Biblico Cristiano Online y Gratis en Español - Significado o definición de Drusila 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Drusilla=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Drusilla (38-25 August 79 AD) was a daughter of Herod Agrippa I and thus sister to Berenice, Mariamne and Herod Agrippa II. She perished in the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in AD 79.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT She was six years of age at the time of her father's death at Caesarea in 44. Her father had betrothed her to Gaius Julius Archelaus Antiochus Epiphanes, first son of King Antiochus IV of Commagene, with a stipulation from her fat
2 CONC her that Epiphanes should embrace the Jewish religion. The prince in the end refused to abide by his promise to do so, and the marriage had still not been contracted on her father's death. On Agrippa's death:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''...the inhabitants of Caesarea and of Sebaste forgot the kindnesses he had bestowed on them, and acted the part of the bitterest enemies; for they cast such reproaches upon the deceased as are not fit to be spoken of; and so man
2 CONC y of them as were then soldiers, which were a great number, went to his house, and hastily carried off the statues of [Agrippa I]'s daughters, and all at once carried them into the brothels, and when they had set them on the broth
2 CONC el roofs, they abused them to the utmost of their power, and did such things to them as are too indecent to be related. They also laid themselves down in public places, and celebrated general feastings, with garlands on their head
2 CONC s, and with ointments and libations to Charon, and drinking to one another for joy that the king was expired, not only unmindful of Agrippa, who had extended his liberality to them in abundance, but also of his grandfather Herod t
2 CONC he Great, who had himself rebuilt their cities, and had raised them havens and temples at vast expense.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Once Drusilla's brother, Herod Agrippa II, had been assigned the tetrachy of Herod Philip I (along with Batanea, Trachonites and Abila) in around 49/50, he broke off her engagement to Epiphanes and gave her in marriage to Gaius Ju
2 CONC lius Azizus, Priest King of Emesa, who, in order to obtain her hand, consented to be circumcised. Herod also at around this time married Mariamne to her betrothed, Gaius the prince of Commagene.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Marriage to Antonius Felix''
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that it was shortly after her first marriage was contracted that Antonius Felix, the Roman procurator of Judea, met Drusilla, probably at her brother's court (Berenice, the elder sister, lived with her brother at this t
2 CONC ime, and thus Drusilla probably did too). Felix was struck by the great beauty of Drusilla, and determined to make her his (second) wife. In order to persuade her, a practising Jew, to divorce her Jewish husband and marry him, a p
2 CONC agan, he took the following steps:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''While Felix was procurator of Judea, he saw this Drusilla, and fell in love with her; for she did indeed exceed all other women in beauty; and he sent to her a person whose name was Simon[4] (Note: in some manuscripts, Atomos), 
2 CONC a Jewish friend of his, by birth a Cypriot, who pretended to be a magician. Simon endeavored to persuade her to forsake her present husband, and marry Felix; and promised, that if she would not refuse Felix, he would make her a ha
2 CONC ppy woman. Accordingly she acted unwisely and, because she longed to avoid her sister Berenice's envy (for Drusilla was very ill-treated by Berenice because of Drusilla's beauty) was prevailed upon to transgress the laws of her fo
2 CONC refathers, and to marry Felix.''
2 CONT
2 CONT She was about nineteen years of age when she appeared at Felix's side, during St. Paul's captivity at Caesarea - the Book of Acts 24:24 reports her thus:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''"Several days later Felix came [back into court] with his wife Drusilla, who was a Jewess."''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Book of Acts gives no further information on her subsequent life, though Josephus states that they had a son named Marcus Antonius Agrippa and a daughter Antonia Clementiana. Their son perished together with Drusilla, along wi
2 CONC th noted Roman historian Pliny the Elder plus most of the populations of Pompeii and Herculaneum in the AD 79 eruption of Mount Vesuvius.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drusilla_(daughter_of_Herod_Agrippa)
2 CONT
2 CONT †††† Bible source⬇️
2 CONT
2 CONT Acts 24:24The Verdict Postponed
2 CONT …23He ordered the centurion to keep Paul under guard, but to allow him some freedom and permit his friends to minister to his needs. 24After several days, Felix returned with his wife Drusilla, who was a Jewess. He sent for Paul a
2 CONC nd listened to him speak about faith in Christ Jesus. 25As Paul expounded on righteousness, self-control, and the coming judgment, Felix became frightened and said, “You may go for now. When I find the time, I will call for you.”…
