1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_I_of_Macedon
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 515 B.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 450 B.C. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Macedonia Alexander I Philhellene Argaead was the son of King of Macedonia Amyntas I Argaead .1,2 He fought in the army of Xerxes with a Macedonian contingent during the Persian wars 0484 B.C..3,4 He was elected as king to
2 CONC  succeed his father 0495 B.C. In circa.5 King of Macedonia, Persian Empire, 0495-0452 B.C..5 He was honored by a poem from Pindar, which addressed him as "bold-scheming son of Amyntas".5 He managed to secretly help the Greeks agai
2 CONC nst the Persians, earning the surname "Philhellen", that is, "friend of the Greeks," and as a result, obtained for Macedon the freedom from Persian dominion after the victory of the Greeks.4 A bronze tripod, with an inscription on
2 CONC  it's rim, showing that it was won in the Heraia games held at Argos, was found in the Royal Macedonian tombs. The inscription, by it's lettering dated to 430-420 B.C., read: "I am from the games of the Argive Hera." While not def
2 CONC iniate that is Alexander I's prize, it shows that some Macedonian king participated in the games.6 Sources: 1. Hammond, N.G.L. and Griffith, G.T. 'A History of Macedonia' Vol.II, chart between pp.176-177. "Men of Athens... Had I n
2 CONC ot greatly at heart the common welfare of Hellas I should not have come to tell you; but I am myself Hellene by descent, and I would not willingly see Hellas exchange freedom for slavery.... If you prosper in this war, forget not 
2 CONC to do something for my freedom; consider the risk I have run, out of zeal for the Hellenic cause, to acquaint you with what Mardonius intends, and to save you from being surprised by the barbarians. I am Alexander of Macedon." (He
2 CONC rodotus, The Histories, 9.45).7 He was the first Macedonian to take part in the Olympic games.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of King of Macedonia Alexander I Philhellene Argaead:
2 CONT
2 CONT Amyntas II Argaead+
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Macedonia Perdiccas II Argaead + d. 0413 B.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S204] Roderick W. Stuart, Royalty for Commoners: The Complete Lineage of John of Gaunt, Son of Edward III, Kings of England, and Queen Philippa (.: ., 3rd Ed., 1998), 413-85. Hereinafter cited as RfC.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S723] Herodotus of Halicarnassus, The History of Herodotus (London and New York: MacMillan and Co., 1890). Hereinafter cited as Herodotus' History.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S204] Roderick W. Stuart, RfC, 413-84.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S1078] Plato and his dialogues, online . Hereinafter cited as Plato and his dialogues.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S669] M. B. Sakellariou, Macedonia, 4000 Years of Greek History and Civilization, Greek Lands in History (8, Philadelphias Street, Athens, Greece: Ekdotike Athenon S.A., 1983/1988), pg. 68. Hereinafter cited as Sakellariou.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S959] Manolis Andronicos, Vergina: The Royal Tombs (Athens, Greece: Ekdotike Hellados S.A., 1989), pg. 164-166. Hereinafter cited as Vergina.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S723] Herodotus of Halicarnassus, Herodotus' History, 9.45. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alejandro I  El Filoheleno; Dinastía argénida Rey de Epiro aproximadamente 342 aC, el hermano de Olimpia, la madre de Alejandro Magno, y el hijo-en-ley de Filipo de Macedonia, cuya hija se casó con Cleopatra (336). En 322 cruzó a 
2 CONC Italia para asistir a los Tarentinos contra los lucanos, brucios y samnitas. Obtuvo un éxito considerable e hizo un arreglo con los romanos para un ataque conjunto sobre los samnitas; pero los Tarentinos, sospechando del diseño de
2 CONC  fundar un reino independiente, se volvieron contra él. Aunque la ventaja en un principio correspondía a Alexander, que perdió poco a poco, y sus partidarios se redujo de distancia. En 330 (o anterior) fue derrotado en Pandosia y 
2 CONC asesinado por un emigrante Lucania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enciclopedia Britanica - 1911
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argead_dynasty
2 CONT
