1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gygaea of Macedon For other uses, see Gygaea (disambiguation). Gygaea was a daughter of Amyntas I and sister of Alexander I of Macedon. She was given away in marriage by her brother to the Persian official Bubares.
2 CONC [1] Herodotus also mentions a son of Bubares and Gygaea, called Amyntas, who was later given the city Alabanda in Caria by Xerxes I (r. 486-465).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also another Gygaea, second wife of Amyntas III of Macedon, whose son Menelaus was put to death by his stepbrother Philip II in 347 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT References	Edit
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Roisman & Worthington, p. 343. ^ Roisman & Worthington, p. 136. Sources	Edit
2 CONT
2 CONT Carney, Elizabeth Donnelly (2000), Women and monarchy in Macedonia, Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, p. 46, ISBN 0-8061-3212-4 Roisman, Joseph; Worthington, Ian (2011). A Companion to Ancient Macedonia. John Wiley and Sons. I
2 CONC SBN 978-1-44-435163-7.
2 CONT This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Smith, William, ed. (1870). "article name needed". Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology.
2 CONT
