1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Nabonidus I of Babylon
2 CONT *Prefix: King
2 CONT *Given Name: Nabonidus I
2 CONT *Surname: of Babylon
2 CONT *Sex: M
2 CONT * Father: Nabubalatsuigbi of Harran 
2 CONT * Mother: Addaguppi
2 CONT * Marriage 1 Nitokkris of Babylon
2 CONT
2 CONT Children include
2 CONT #Nebuchadnezzar III of Babylon
2 CONT # Belshazzar
2 CONT # Ennigaldi-Nanna
2 CONT #Ina-Esagila-remat
2 CONT #Akkabuʾunma
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabonidus
2 CONT
2 CONT Nabonidus (Babylonian cuneiform:   Nabû-naʾid,[2][3] meaning "May Nabu be exalted"[3] or "Nabu is praised")[4] was the last king of the Neo-Babylonian Empire, ruling from 556 BC to the fall of Babylon to the Achaemenian Empire und
2 CONC er Cyrus the Great in 539 BC. Nabonidus was the last native ruler of ancient Mesopotamia,[5][6] the end of his reign marking the end of thousands of years of Sumero-Akkadian states, kingdoms and empires. He was also the last indep
2 CONC endent king of Babylon.[a] Regarded as one of the most vibrant and individualistic rulers of his time, Nabonidus is characterised by some scholars as an unorthodox religious reformer and as the first archaeologist.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origins of Nabonidus, his connection to previous royalty, and subsequently what claim he had to the throne remain unclear, given that Nabonidus made no genealogical claims of kinship to previous kings. This suggests that he wa
2 CONC s neither related nor connected to the Chaldean dynasty of Babylonian rulers. However, he is known to have had a prominent career of some kind before he became king. It is possible that he was connected to the Chaldean kings via m
2 CONC arriage, possibly having married a daughter of Nebuchadnezzar II (r. 605–562 BC). Nabonidus's mother, Adad-guppi, was of Assyrian ancestry. His father, Nabu-balatsu-iqbi, of whom little is known, may also have been either Assyrian
2 CONC  or Babylonian. Some historians have speculated that either Adad-guppi or Nabu-balatsu-iqbi were members of the Sargonid dynasty, rulers of the Neo-Assyrian Empire until its fall in 609 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Rise to the throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Nabonidus rose to the throne in the aftermath of the collapse of the direct dynastic line of the Chaldean dynasty. After the brief reign of Neriglissar, a son-in-law of Nebuchadnezzar II, the throne had been inherited in April 556
2 CONC  BC by Neriglissar's son Labashi-Marduk.[11] Berossus erroneously wrote that Labashi-Marduk ruled for nine months (though this might be attributable to a scribal error) and stated that Labashi-Marduk's "evil ways" led to his frien
2 CONC ds plotting against him, eventually resulting in the "child king" being beaten to death. The plotters then agreed that Nabonnedos (Nabonidus), one of the plotters, should rule.[34] The reason for the coup against Labashi-Marduk is
2 CONC  unknown. It is possible that despite Labashi-Marduk and his father being well-connected and wealthy, they were ultimately seen as commoners, lacking noble blood.[35] Though Labashi-Marduk may have been the grandson of Nebuchadnez
2 CONC zar II through his mother, making him part of the royal bloodline, it is also possible he was the son of Neriglissar and a different wife. Thus, Labashi-Marduk's rise to the throne might have signified a true break in the dynasty 
2 CONC of Nebuchadnezzar II and might as such have aroused opposition from the Babylonian populace.[13] Although Berossus refers to Labashi-Marduk as a child, it is possible that he became king as an adult since commercial texts from two
2 CONC  years earlier indicate that Labashi-Marduk was in charge of his own affairs at that time.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Though Nabonidus in his inscriptions claims that he had few supporters and that he did not covet the throne himself, he must have been a leading figure in the conspiracy that led to the deposition and death of Labashi-Marduk.[12] 
2 CONC Nabonidus enjoyed consistent support from the Babylonian military throughout his reign and it is possible that the army played a role in his rise to the throne.[17] 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Late reign and fall of Babylon
2 CONT
2 CONT Cyrus the Great, who conquered Babylon in 539 BC, depicted with a Hemhem crown, or four-winged Cherub tutelary divinity
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000205597594822&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT >Source: < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabonidus#/media/File:Illustrerad_Verldshistoria_band_I_Ill_058.jpg Wikimedia] > (public domain)
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 October,[96] the Persian army, led by the governor Ugbaru, entered Babylon without a fight.[95] The last tablet dated to Nabonidus's reign is from Uruk and is dated to 13 October, which is usually considered the end date of 
2 CONC his reign.[96] Shield-bearing Persian troops were assigned to guard the temples of Babylon, so that priests in safety could continue their services and rituals. On 29[97] or 30[95] October, Cyrus himself entered Babylon as its new
2 CONC  king. He received the acclamation of the people, though whether it was as a liberator from oppression, as Cyrus presented himself, or as a conqueror, is open to interpretation.[95] The end of Nabonidus's reign is sometimes altern
2 CONC atively dated to Babylon's fall to the Persians on 12 October, a day earlier than the last tablet dated to Nabonidus's reign, or to Cyrus's entry into the city, when Cyrus formally became king.[98]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fate
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancient accounts differ as to the fate of Nabonidus after the fall of Babylon. The 5th/4th-century BC Greek historian Xenophon wrote that Ugbaru (or 'Gobryas') killed Nabonidus upon the capture of Babylon, but it is possible that 
2 CONC Xenophon meant Belshazzar, whose death at the fall of Babylon is also recorded in the Biblical Book of Daniel.[1] Berossus wrote that Nabonidus surrendered to Cyrus at Borsippa after the fall of Babylon who dealt with him "in a gr
2 CONC acious manner", sparing his life and allowing him to retire, or possibly appointing him to be a governor, in Carmania (approximately the modern Kerman Province in Iran), where Nabonidus lived out the rest of his life.[95][1] The r
2 CONC oyal chronicle simply states that Nabonidus was captured in Babylon after retreating, leaving his subsequent fate unclear.[1] The Dynastic Prophecy corroborates Berossus's account, by stating that Nabonidus was removed from his th
2 CONC rone and settled "in another land".[95][1] If Berossus is believed, Nabonidus lived into the reign of Darius the Great (r. 522–486 BC), outliving both Cyrus and Cyrus's son and successor Cambyses II, given that Berossus claims tha
2 CONC t "King Darius, however, took away a part of his province for himself". Given the age of his mother at the time of her death, it is not impossible that Nabonidus too would have lived for over a century.[1]
2 CONT The fate of Belshazzar is unknown, given that none of the sources describe what happened to him.[99] It is typically assumed that Belshazzar was killed by the Persians at Babylon when the city fell, on 12 October,[1][95] though he
2 CONC  may alternatively already have been killed at the battle of Opis,[27] captured and executed, or exiled together with his father.[99]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible as well that Nabonidus married one of Nebuchadnezzar II's daughters, a marriage which could potentially have been secured through his mother's influence. Not only would such a connection explain Nabonidus's rise to 
2 CONC the throne (being connected to the royal family) but it would also explain later historical traditions in which Nabonidus's son, Belshazzar, is described as Nebuchadnezzar II's descendant; as in the Book of Daniel in the Hebrew Bi
2 CONC ble, where Belshazzar is referred to as Nebuchadnezzar II's (grand)son.[17] The claim of Belshazzar being Nebuchadnezzar II's descendant however, could also alternatively derive from royal propaganda, rather than true genealogical
2 CONC  information.[27] The ancient Greek historian Herodotus names the "last great queen" of the Babylonian Empire as Nitocris, but neither that name, nor any other name, is attested in contemporary Babylonian sources. Herodotus's desc
2 CONC ription of Nitocris contains a wealth of legendary material making it difficult to determine whether he uses the name to refer to Nabonidus's wife or mother. William H. Shea proposed in 1982 that Nitocris may tentatively be identi
2 CONC fied as the name of Nabonidus's wife and Belshazzar's mother.[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT Details on Nabonidus's family are scarce. He likely had a large family even prior to becoming king, seeing as his mother Adad-guppi in her inscriptions claims that she had great-great-grandchildren,[26] and Nabonidus was presumabl
2 CONC y Adad-guppi's only child.[12] Adad-guppi having great-great-grandchildren means that Nabonidus would have had great-grandchildren early in his reign, though the names, lineage, number and genders of these descendants are not ment
2 CONC ioned.[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The known children of Nabonidus are:[17][24][27][53][100]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Belshazzar (Akkadian: Bēl-šar-uṣur)[27] – a son. Crown prince throughout Nabonidus's reign and regent 553–543/542 BC.[53]
2 CONT # Ennigaldi-Nanna (Akkadian: En-nigaldi-Nanna)[24] – a daughter. Consecrated by her father as an entum-priestess in Ur.[17]
2 CONT # Ina-Esagila-remat[100] or Ina-Esagila-risat[24] (Akkadian: Ina-Esagil-rīšat)[24] – a daughter. Mentioned as the recipient of a tithe at Sippar,[100] otherwise little is known of her.[17]
2 CONT # Akkabuʾunma (Akkadian: Akkabuʾunma, exact reading uncertain)[24] – a daughter. Recorded in archival texts at Sippar.[100]
2 CONT # Possibly another daughter recorded in archival texts at Sippar, however, her name is unknown.[100]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Character and remembrance'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT No other Neo-Babylonian king has been characterised in as varied a manner as Nabonidus.[105] Though some Classical authors forgot, or omitted, details of Nabonidus in their accounts of Babylonia, only preserving him as a name in t
2 CONC heir lists of kings, the Achaemenid Empire that succeeded the reign of Nabonidus in Babylonia viewed his rule as an example that should never be emulated. The Achaemenids did not consider Nabonidus's more traditional work, restori
2 CONC ng temples etc., to be important, but emphasised in their historiography the points when Nabonidus went contrary to what was expected of a Babylonian king. Cyrus the Great justified his conquest of Babylon by presenting himself as
2 CONC  a champion divinely ordained by Marduk and by writing accounts of Nabonidus's "heretical" acts.[106]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Nebuchadnezzar (1795) by William Blake. The painting depicts Nebuchadnezzar II as nude and mad, living like a wild animal. The story of Nebuchadnezzar II's madness originally referred to Nabonidus.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000205596877882&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT >Source: < [https://blakearchive.org/search/?search=Nebuchadnezzar The William Blake Archive] > (public domain)
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Babylon, a legend of Nabonidus having been mad, on account of his religious policies, gradually formed, which would eventually find its way into Hellenistic and Jewish tradition.[107] In the Book of Daniel, Nabon
2 CONC idus's supposed madness is reattributed to Nebuchadnezzar II.[108] The accusation of insanity is not found in any cuneiform sources. The Verse Account is highly critical of Nabonidus, especially his religious policies, and though 
2 CONC it presents Cyrus the Great as a liberator rather than conqueror, it makes no direct claim that Nabonidus was insane. The Dynastic Prophecy and the Cyrus Cylinder offer similar accounts, criticising Nabonidus and his policies, but
2 CONC  not characterising him as mad.[108] Some Babylonian sources are more neutral. The Babylonian Chronicle, a more objective account of Babylonian history, records the king as being absent from Babylon for years on end, and the resul
2 CONC ting suspension of the New Year's festival, but does not pass any judgement on these events. In his history of Babylonia, Berossus presents Nabonidus as a usurper, which Nabonidus himself admitted to being, but reports nothing tha
2 CONC t could be construed as negative assessment of the king. As such, it is clear that contemporary views of Nabonidus were not completely negative. Had the Babylonians universally dismissed Nabonidus as an incompetent and impious her
2 CONC etic, it is also unlikely that two later Babylonian rebels would have claimed to be his sons. Cuneiform sources suggest that the Babylonians remembered Nabonidus as unorthodox and misguided, but not insane.[109]
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
