2 TITL Family tree of the Chaldean dynasty
1 NOTE He reigned 605-562 bc, greatest king of the neo-Babylonian, or Chaldean, 
2 CONT dynasty, who conquered much of southwestern Asia; known also for his 
2 CONT extensive building in the major cities of Babylonia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nebuchadnezzar commanded a Babylonian army late in his father's reign and 
2 CONT in 605 bc triumphed over Egyptian forces at the decisive Battle of 
2 CONT Carchemish in Syria, which made Babylonia the primary military power in 
2 CONT the Middle East. After his father's death, Nebuchadnezzar returned to 
2 CONT Babylon and ascended the throne on September 7, 605 bc. During the next 
2 CONT eight years he campaigned extensively in the west against Syria, 
2 CONT Palestine, and Egypt and against the Arabs. On March 16, 597 bc, he 
2 CONT captured Jerusalem and took Jehoiakim, king of Judah, and many of his 
2 CONT people captive to Babylonia. He was subsequently troubled by major revolts 
2 CONT in Babylonia (594 bc) and in Judah (588-587 bc), which were vigorously 
2 CONT punished; many more Jews were exiled to Babylonia. Nebuchadnezzar also 
2 CONT conducted a 13-year siege of the Phoenician city of Tyre and launched an 
2 CONT invasion of Egypt in 568 bc. During the latter part of his reign, as the 
2 CONT empire of the Medes increased in power to the north and east, 
2 CONT Nebuchadnezzar built a wall, known as the Median Wall, in northern 
2 CONT Babylonia to keep out the potential invader. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nebuchadnezzar's conquests brought in much booty and tribute, creating an 
2 CONT age of prosperity for Babylonia. He undertook an ambitious construction 
2 CONT program, rebuilding the temples in the major cult cities and refurbishing 
2 CONT his capital at Babylon with a splendid ziggurat (pyramid temple) as well 
2 CONT as other shrines, palaces, fortification walls, and processional ways. 
2 CONT Later legend credited him with building one of the Seven Wonders of the 
2 CONT Ancient World, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, for his Median wife 
2 CONT Amyitis. Nebuchadnezzar died in early October 562 bc and was succeeded by 
2 CONT his son Amel-Marduk (reigned 562-560 bc; the biblical Evil-Merodach).
1 NOTE KING OF BABYLONIA ca. 605-562 BC (DIED 562 BC); CONQUERED JERUSALEM, AND
2 CONT DESTROYED THE TEMPLE; DEPORTED MANY JEWS INTO BABYLONIA 586 BC
