2 CONC Hellenes, providing a fixed point in their calendar. "By the fifth century at least," J.A.S. Evans remarked, "Croesus had become a figure of myth, who stood outside the conventional restraints of chronology."[2] Croesus was renown
2 CONC ed for his wealth—Herodotus and Pausanias noted his gifts preserved at Delphi [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croesus   .  .  .  ''Continued]
