1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Gaius Antonius=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaius Antonius (died 42 BC) was the second son of Marcus Antonius Creticus and Julia Antonia, and thus, younger brother of Mark Antony, triumvir
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his brother, Gaius started his life free from paternal guidance, in the midst of scandals, parties and gambling.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Caesar's civil war''
2 CONT
2 CONT During the civil war, Gaius was a legate of Julius Caesar (49 BC) and was entrusted with the defense of Illyria against Pompeians and with the campaign to regain cities already taken by rebels and the Pompeians. Gaius was entruste
2 CONC d with the newly recruited 24th legion and half of the new 28th legion. On the way to Illyria, Antonius was intercepted by a Pompeian fleet. Led by centurion Titus Pullo, the men refused to fight, and turned traitor. They were the
2 CONC n reported to be fighting at the Battle of Dyrrachium.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Caesar's dictatorship''
2 CONT
2 CONT With all the members of the Antonius family, he was then promoted to high offices of the cursus honorum. In 44 BC, Gaius was urban praetor, while his brothers Mark Antony and Lucius Antonius were consul and tribune respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Caesar's assassination and afterwards''
2 CONT
2 CONT After the assassination of Caesar, Gaius (as a Caesarean) was appointed governor to the Roman province of Macedonia. Marcus Junius Brutus and the other assassins, however, chose Macedonia as refuge from Octavian and - following op
2 CONC position from Gaius - dispossessed him of his governorship. Brutus at first seemed to treat him generously, but, on finding that he was attempting to persuade his troops to mutiny, ultimately ordered his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Antonius_(brother_of_Mark_Antony)
