1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamshi-Adad_IV
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Shamshi-Adad IVFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Šamši-Adad IV, inscribed mdšam-ši-dIM, was the king of Assyria, 1054/3–1050 BC, the 91st to be listed on the Assyrian Kinglist.[i 1][i 2] He was a son
2 CONC  of Tukultī-apil-Ešarra I (1114–1076 BC), the third to have taken the throne, after his brothers Ašarēd-apil-Ekur and Ashur-bel-kala, and he usurped the kingship from the latter’s son, the short-reigning Erība-Adad II (1055–1054 B
2 CONC C). It is quite probable that he was fairly elderly when he seized the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] BiographyThe Assyrian kinglist recalls that he “came up from Karduniaš (i.e. Babylonia). He ousted Erība-Adad, son of Aššur-bêl-kala, seized the throne and ruled for 4 years.” The king of Babylon was Adad-apla-iddina, who h
2 CONC ad been installed more than a decade earlier by Šamši-Adad’s brother, Ashur-bel-kala. The extent to which he was instrumental in the succession is uncertain but it seems that Šamši-Adad may have earlier sought refuge in exile in t
2 CONC he south.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Synchronistic Kinglist[i 3] gives Ea-, presumed to be Ea-mukin-zēri (ca 1008 BC), as his Babylonian contemporary,[2] an unlikely pairing as he was likely to have been concurrent with the latter kings of the 2nd dynasty of Isin
2 CONC  during its dying throws. The political events of his reign are obscure and his fragmentary inscriptions are limited to commemorating renovation work carried out on the Ištar temple at Nineveh and the bīt nāmeru, “gate-tower,” at 
2 CONC Aššur.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He would be succeeded by his son, Aššur-naṣir-apli I.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Inscriptions1.^ Khorsabad Kinglist, tablet IM 60017 (excavation nos.: DS 828, DS 32-54), iv 1–4. 2.^ SDAS Kinglist, tablet IM 60484, iii 33–36. 3.^ Synchronistic Kinglist, Ass 14616c (KAV 216), iii 3. [edit] References1.^ J
2 CONC . A. Brinkman (1968). A Political History of Post-Kassite Babylonia, 1158–722 BC. Pontificium Institutum Biblicum. pp. 143–144. 2.^ Heather D. Baker (2008). "Šamši-Adad IV". Reallexikon der Assyriologie: Prinz, Prinzessin - Samug,
2 CONC  Bd. 11. Walter De Gruyter. p. 636. 3.^ D. J. Wiseman (1975). "XXXI: Assyria & Babylonia 1200–1000 BC". In I. E. S. Edwards, C. J. Gadd, N. G. L. Hammond, S. Solberger. The Cambridge Ancient History, Volume II, Part 2, History of 
2 CONC the Middle East and the Aegean Region, 1380–1000 BC. Cambridge University Press. p. 469.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Erība-Adad II King of Assyria 1054–1050 BC Succeeded by Aššur-naṣir-apli I  [show]v ·t ·eAssyrian kings 
2 CONT
