1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ashur-nirari IVFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search   Schroeder’s line art for the KAV 21 list of Eponyms showing the six years of Aššur-nērārī IV and both his predecessor and successor.
2 CONC [i 1]Aššur-nērārī IV, inscribed maš-šur-ERIM.GABA, "(the god) Aššur is my help,"[1] was the king of Assyria, the 94th to appear on the Assyrian Kinglist,[i 2][i 3] ruling 1019/18-1013 BC. His short six-year reign was marked by con
2 CONC fusion and a dearth of contemporary inscriptions.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] BiographyHe succeeded his father, Salmānu-ašarēd II, whose twelve-year reign seems to have ended in confusion, as the last limmu official on his eponym list[i 1] is missing and recorded as ša ar[ki si...], the eponym ‘which
2 CONC  is after’ (the previous name). Aššur-nērārī took the eponymy during his first year but the following year is marked ša EGIR maš-šur-, “(year) after Aššur-…” and thereafter all the remaining years were recorded with a sequential n
2 CONC umber and a Winkelhaken to designate “ditto.” It is probable that events were so turbulent during this period that an eponym was not appointed.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Babylonian king, Ninurta-kudurrῑ-uṣur I (987 – 985 BC) is given as his counterpart on the Synchronistic Kinglist[i 4] but the conventional chronology would suggest it was the earlier monarch, Simbar-Šipak (1025-1008 BC). The l
2 CONC ater king, Aššur-nāṣir-apli II mentions "Sibir, king of Karduniaš" in the context of the capture of the city of Atlila, in his annals, and historians have tentatively identified this individual with Simbar-Šipak, suggesting he eng
2 CONC aged in warfare against Assyria around this time.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT His successor was his uncle, Aššur-rabi II, a younger son of the earlier king Aššur-nāṣir-apli I. The circumstances of the succession are unknown and the Assyrian Kinglist gives no indication that he was overthrown, the usual caus
2 CONC e of an uncle to succeed his nephew in the Assyrian monarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashur-nirari_IV
