1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Anna''', who married Hmayeak, a Mamikonian prince (died c. 797), by whom she had Konstantinos, an officer at the court of [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael-III-Byzantine-Emperor/6000000003051267350 Emperor Mi
2 CONC chael III].[17][18]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT siblings:                                                                                                                                      
2 CONT *Sybatios Gnuni  800-> 847
2 CONT *Basilios Gnuni  805-> 847
2 CONT *Gregorius Gnuni  808-865
2 CONT *Theodosius Gnuni  810-????
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas Adontz in his book The age and origins of the emperor Basil I (1933) expressed a theory that Leo V and Theodosia were ancestors of [https://www.geni.com/people/Basil-I-Byzantine-Emperor/6000000002434099128 Basil I].  The 
2 CONC theory was partly based on the account of his ancestry given by Constantine VII, a grandson of Basil I, as well as the accounts given by Theophanes Continuatus.[15] Basil I, according to these accounts, was a son of peasants. His 
2 CONC mother is named by Constantine VII as "Pankalo". The name of his father was not recorded, but the names Symbatios and Constantine have been suggested; both were names used by the eldest sons of Basil, with eldest sons of Byzantine
2 CONC s typically named after their grandfathers.[15] The paternal grandfather of Basil is named as Maiactes. The paternal grandmother was not named but was identified as a daughter of "Leo", a citizen of Constantinople. Adontz identifi
2 CONC ed this Leo as Leo V, which would make Leo V and Theodosia great-grandparents of Basil I.[15] Adontz also suggested Constantine VII had made a mistake in the generations separating Maiactes and Basil. This suggests that Basil was 
2 CONC a great-grandson of Maiactes and not old enough to have seen the wars with Krum of Bulgaria, which would make Leo V and Theodosia fourth-generation ancestors of Basil.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT The theory has been accepted by several genealogists, including Christian Settipani in his search for descent from antiquity. The name "Anna" has been suggested for the daughter of Leo V and Theodosia, because it was given to daug
2 CONC hters of Basil I, Leo VI the Wise, Constantine VII and Romanos II--almost every emperor that would claim descent from this woman.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * http://erwan.gil.free.fr/modules/freepages/pharaons/ramses_II.pdf page 13 - 14
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_V_the_Armenian
2 CONT * http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM.htm#LeonVdied820B not listed by Cawley
2 CONT * https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00270692&tree=LEO called N.N., cites 
2 CONT * Nos Ancêtres de l'Antiquité Paris, 1991 , Settipani, Christian. 187 
2 CONT --------------------
