0 @N6000000002187617585@ NOTE Mithridates II of Pontus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates II (in Greek M????d?t??; lived 3rd century BC), third king of Pontus and son of Ariobarzanes, whom he succeeded on the throne. He was a minor when his father died, but the period of his accession cannot be deter­mined.
1 CONC  It seems probable that it must be placed considerably before 240 BC, as Memnon tells us that he was a child at his father's death, and he had a daughter of marriageable age in 222 BC. Shortly after his accession, his kingdom was 
1 CONC invaded by the Gauls, who were eventually repulsed.1 After he attained manhood, he married Laodice, a sister of Seleucus II Callinicus, with whom he is said to have received the province of Phrygia as a dowry.2 But notwithstanding
1 CONC  this alliance, we find Mithridates II fighting against Seleucus during a war between Seleucus and Antiochus Hierax. Eventually, Mithridates defeated Seleucus in a great battle whereby Seleucus lost twenty thousand of his troops a
1 CONC nd narrowly escaped with his own life.3 In 222 BC, Mithridates gave his daughter Laodice in marriage to the Seleucid king Antiochus III: another of his daughters, also named Laodice, was married about the same time to Achaeus, the
1 CONC  cousin of Antiochus.4 In 220 BC, Mithridates declared war upon the wealthy and powerful city of Sinope. However, he was unable to weaken it and the city did not fall into the power of the kings of Pontus until 183 BC.5 At an earl
1 CONC ier period, we find Mithridates II vying with the other monarchs of Asia in sending magnificent presents to the Rhodians, after the subversion of their city by an earthquake in 227 BC.6 The date of his death is utterly unknown. He
1 CONC  was succeeded by his son Mithridates III.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ariobarzanes King of Pontus
1 CONT c. 250 BC – c. 210 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mithridates III
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hazel, John. Who's Who in the Greek World. "Mithridates II", 2003.
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Mithridates IV", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Memnon, History of Heracleia, 24
1 CONT 2 Eusebius of Caesarea, Chronicon (Schoene ed.), pag. 251; Justin, Epitome, xxxviii. 5
1 CONT 3 Eusebius, Ibid.
1 CONT 4 Polybius, Histories, v. 43, 74, viii. 22
1 CONT 5 Ibid., iv. 56
1 CONT 6 Ibid., v. 90
0 @N6000000001353851908@ NOTE Ruled 67 BC, King of Media Atropatene
1 CONT Event: Ruled 57 - 54 BC, King of Parthia (Pretender) 1
1 CONT Note: Mithradates III murdered his father Phraates about 57 B.C., with the assistance of his brother Orodes. He was made king of Media andwaged war against his brother, but was soon deposed on account of hiscruelty. He took refuge
1 CONC  with Gabinius, the Roman proconsul of Syria. He advanced into Mesopotamia, but was beaten at Seleucia by Surenas, fled into Babylon and after a long siege was taken prisoner and killed in 54 by his brother  Orodes I.
0 @N6000000001354419044@ NOTE Birth: in c. 220 - 185 BC 1
1 CONT Event: Ancestor M
1 CONT Event: Ruled 250 - 220 BC, King of Pontus 1
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Mithradates III was very young when he succeeded his father, Ariobarzanes, c. 255 B.C. Early in his reign the Gauls (people of Galatia] invaded Pontus. Mithradates was at the battle of Ancyra (c. 240), in which he assisted Antioch
1 CONC us Hierax against his brother Seleucus Callinicus. His wife, their sister Laodice, had brought him Greater Phrygia as her dowry; and his daughter, also named Laodice, married Antiochus theGreat. He attacked Sinope (c. 220), but it
1 CONC  was not taken until after his death (c. 185). His son and successor Pharnaces I took Sinope (which he made the capital of the kingdom instead of Amasia) and tried also to extend his power into the Chersonesus Taurica
