0 @N6000000002187600454@ NOTE Zariadres
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Zariadres I was King of Sophene.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 201 BC Antiochus III the Great conquers Greater Armenia and Sophene with his Armenian generals(strategoi) Artaxias and Zariadres. Antiochus appointed Zariadres as satrap of Sophene. Following the defeat of Antiochus by the Roma
1 CONC ns at the Battle of Magnesia in 190 BC, Artaxias and Zariadres revolted and, with Roman consent, began to reign autonomously; Artaxias over Greater Armenia and Zariadres over Sophene.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Iranian mythology, Zariadres was the younger brother of Hystaspes, and the hero of the celebrated lovestory of Zariadres and Odatis.
0 @N6000000002187617506@ NOTE Ariarathes VI of Cappadocia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ariarathes VI Epiphanes Philopator (in Greek A??a????? Ep?fa??? F??op?t??; reigned 130–116 BC), king of Cappadocia, was the youngest son of Ariarathes V. He reigned about 14 years (130–116 BC). He was a child at his succession, an
1 CONC d for this reason the power was kept by his mother Nysa. At some point her mother seems to have poisoned all Ariarathes five brothers; but the infant king was saved by people loyal to the dynasty and Nysa killed. These facts were 
1 CONC a good pretext for Mithridates Euergetes (151–120 BC), king of Pontus, for trying to assert control over the country. For this he married Ariarathes to Laodice, Mithridates daughter; but since this wasn't deemed enough to trasform
1 CONC  Cappadocia in a satellite of Pontus, Mithridates Euergetes' son, Mithridates VI, murdered Ariarathes using Gordius, a Cappadocian nobleman.1 On his death the kingdom was briefly ruled by Ariarathes' widow and then seized by Nicom
1 CONC edes III, king of Bithynia, who married Laodice the widow of the late king, Laodice. But Nicomedes III was soon expelled by Mithridates VI, who placed upon the throne Ariarathes VII, a son of Ariarathes VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ariarathes V King of Cappadocia
1 CONT 130 BC – 116 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Ariarathes VII
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hazel, John; Who's Who in the Greek World, "Ariarathes VI", (1999)
1 CONT     * Head, Barclay; Historia Numorum, "Cappadocia", (1911)
1 CONT     * Justin; Epitome of Pompeius Trogus, John Selby Watson (translator); London, (1886)
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Ariarathes VI", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Note
0 @N6000000001353854031@ NOTE Birth: in bef. 150 BC
1 CONT Death: in 120 BC, Sinope, Pontus [assassinated] 1
1 CONT Event: Ancestor M
1 CONT Event: Ruled 150 - 120 BC, King of Pontus 1
1 CONT Note: Mithradates V Euergetes, the son of Pharnaces, appears to have become sole ruler of Pontus c. 150. At first a friend of the Romans, he sent them a contingent during the Third Punic War and helped them to get possession of Pe
1 CONC rgamum (bequeathed to them by Attalus III, but claimed by Aristonicus, a natural son of Eumenes II). Nicomedes II of Bithynia also helped the Romans in the affair; and both he and Mithradates expected to be rewarded with Phrygia. 
1 CONC A commission awarded it to Mithradates, but the Roman senate refused to ratify the bargain on theground of bribery. Mithradates appears to have taken Phrygia without waiting for a new award. He likewise invaded Cappadocia and marr
1 CONC ied his daughter to the young king Ariarathes VI Epiphanes. He acquired thesuccession of Paphlagonia from its last king, Pylaemenes, and obtained a kind of protectorate over Galatia. At the height of his power, in121 or 120 B.C., 
1 CONC he was assassinated by his courtiers during a banquet in his palace at Sinope.
