0 @N6000000002187617517@ NOTE Mithridates V of Pontus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates V Euergetes (in Greek M????d?t?? E?e???t??; reigned c. 150–120 BC), seventh king of Pontus, was probably son of Pharnaces I, and nephew of Mithridates III.1 The period of his accession is uncertain. He continued the po
1 CONC litics of alliance with the Romans started by his predecessor. He supported them with some ships and a small auxiliary force during the Third Punic War (149–146 BC)2 and at a subsequent period rendered them useful assistance in th
1 CONC e war against Aristonicus (131–129 BC). For his services on this oc­casion he was rewarded by the consul Manius Aquillius with the province of Phrygia. However, the acts of Aquil­lius were rescinded by the senate on the grounds of
1 CONC  bribery, but it appears that Mithridates maintained his possession of Phrygia until his death.3 He also increased the power of his kingdom by the marriage of his daughter Laodice to the king of Cappadocia Ariarathes VI. The end o
1 CONC f his reign can only be approximately determined based on statements concerning the accession of his son. This assigns the year 120 BC to the end of Mithridates' reign. He was assassinated at Sinope by a conspiracy among his own i
1 CONC mmediate attendants, which possibly included members of his own family.4
1 CONT
1 CONT On the cultural side, his reign was signed by strong hellenizing tendencies; he is recorded for his generous donations to Athens and Delos and the great veneration in which he kept the Greek god Apollo.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Mithridates IV King of Pontus
1 CONT c. 150 BC – 120 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mithridates VI
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hazel, John; Who's Who in the Roman World, "Mithridates V Euergetes", (2002)
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Mithridates V", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
