0 @N6000000002187624176@ NOTE Berenice II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Berenice II (267 or 266 BC - 221 BC), was the daughter of Magas of Cyrene and Queen Apama, and the wife of Ptolemy III Euergetes I, the third ruler of the Ptolemaic dynasty of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT In about 249 BC, she was married to Demetrius the Fair, a Macedonian prince, soon after her father died. However after coming to Cyrene he became the lover of her mother Apama. In a dramatic event, She had him killed in Apamsa's b
1 CONC edroom, but Apama lived on afterwards. This happened around 255 or 250 BC. She had no children with Demetrius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Afterwards she married Ptolemy III. They had at least 4 children: Ptolemy IV, Magas, Arsinoe III and Berenice. She was murdered by Ptolemy IV in 221, soon after he became pharaoh.
1 CONT
1 CONT During her husband's absence on an expedition to Syria, she dedicated her hair to Venus for his safe return, and placed it in the temple of the goddess at Zephyrium. The hair having by some unknown means disappeared, Conon of Samo
1 CONC s, explained the phenomenon in courtly phrase, by saying that it had been carried to the heavens and placed among the stars.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name Coma Berenices or Berenice's hair, applied to a constellation, commemorates this incident. Callimachus celebrated the transformation in a poem, of which only a few lines remain, but there is a fine translation of it by Ca
1 CONC tullus. Soon after her husband's death (221 BC) she was murdered at the instigation of her son Ptolemy IV, with whom she was probably associated in the government.
1 CONT
1 CONT The predecessor of the modern city Benghazi was refounded by her and received her name: Berenice.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Berenice II lived at around 273 B.C.E. and died at approximately 245 B.C.E. She participated in battles and killed several of her enemies. She also raised an army in rebellion against the man that she was betrothed to because she 
1 CONC found him repellent.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Salmonson, Jessica Amanda.(1991) The Encyclopedia of Amazons. Paragon House. Page 33. ISBN 1-55778-420-5
1 CONT     * The House of Ptolemy, Ch. 3
1 CONT     * Berenice II
0 @N6000000002187718751@ NOTE Demetrius II of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius II, son of Antigonus Gonatas, reigned as king of Macedonia from 239 to 229 BC. He belonged to the Antigonid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had already during his father's lifetime distinguished himself by defeating Alexander II of Epirus at Derdia and so saving Macedonia (circa 260 BC). On his accession he had to face a coalition which the two great leagues, usual
1 CONC ly rivals, the Aetolian and Achaean, formed against the Macedonian power. He succeeded in dealing this coalition severe blows, wresting Boeotia from their alliance. The revolution in Epirus, which substituted a republican league f
1 CONC or the monarchy, gravely weakened his position.
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius had also to defend Macedonia against the wild peoples of the north. A battle with the Dardanians turned out disastrously, and he died shortly afterwards, leaving Philip, his son by Chryseis, still a child.
1 CONT
1 CONT Former wives of Demetrius were Stratonice, the daughter of the Seleucid king Antiochus I, Phthia the daughter of Alexander of Epirus, and Nicaea, the widow of his cousin Alexander. The chronology of these marriages is a matter of 
1 CONC dispute.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Antigonus II King of Macedon
1 CONT 239–229 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antigonus III 
0 @N6000000002187718781@ NOTE Ziaelas of Bithynia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ziaelas (reigned c. 254–228 BC), third king of Bithynia, was a son of Nicomedes I and Ditizele. After Nicomedes I died, his second wife Etazeta became ruler on behalf of her infant sons. The grown-up Ziaelas, excluded from the thr
1 CONC one, had previously fled to Armenia. On his father's death he immediately endeavoured to regain his rights by force, he returned, aided by some Galatians. Although Etazeta was supported by neighbouring cities and Antigonus III Dos
1 CONC on, Ziaelas rapidly conquered first part, then all of Bithynia, forcing Etazeta and her sons to escape to Antigonus' court in Macedonia about 254 BC.1 He was succeeded by his son Prusias about 228 BC after being killed by the Gala
1 CONC tian Gauls. 2 Like his father and his grand-father he also founded a new city named after him, Ziela, but the location of the city is unknown.3
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Etazeta King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Prusias I 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Cohen, Getzel M.; The Hellenistic Settlements in Europe, the Islands and Asia Minor (1996), "Ziela" 
1 CONT Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Zeilas", Boston, (1867)
0 @N6000000002187718762@ NOTE Prusias I of Bithynia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prusias I Chlorus (c.230 BC - 182 BC) was the king of Bithynia. The son of Ziaelas, he formed a marriage alliance with Philip V of Macedon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prusias fought a war against Byzantium (220 BC), then defeated the Gauls that Nicomedes I had invited across the Bosporus (218). He expanded the territories of Bithynia in a series of wars against Attalus I of Pergamum and Heracle
1 CONC a Pontica on the Black Sea. Philip V granted him the ports of Cius and Myrleia in 202, which he renamed Prusa and Apameia respectively.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although he granted sanctuary to Hannibal, he remained neutral during Rome's war with Antiochus III the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his son Prusias II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ziaelas King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Prusias II 
0 @N6000000001354417930@ NOTE Mithradates I, also called ARSACES VI (fl. 2nd century BC), king of Parthia (reigned 171-138 BC); he succeeded his brother Phraates I.
1 CONT Before 160 Mithradates I seized Media from the Seleucid ruler Timarchus. Turning to the east, he won two provinces, Tapuria and Traxiana, from the Bactrian king Eucratides. Mithradates then captured the province of Elymais (ancien
1 CONC t Elam) and invaded Babylonia (142 or 141). The Seleucid king Demetrius II Nicator recaptured Babylon (141 or 140) but was defeated and held by Mithradates in honourable captivity. Generally, Mithradates was regarded as a mild rul
1 CONC er, and his epithet Philhellene ("Greek-loving") indicates that he tried to conciliate his Greek subjects.
