0 @N6000000002187617622@ NOTE Mithridates I of Pontus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates I Ctistes (in Greek M????d?t?? ?' Kt?st??; reigned 302 BC - 266 BC) was the founder (this is the meaning of the word Ctistes) of the kingdom of Pontus in Anatolia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 302 or 301 BC, shortly after having executed his father Mithridates of Cius, the diadoch Antigonus became suspicious of the son who had inherited the family dominion of Cius. Antigonus was motivated to form a plan oriented on k
1 CONC illing the boy. Mithridates Ktistes, however, received from Demetrius Poliorcetes timely notice of his father's intentions, and fled with a few followers to Paphlagonia, where he occupied a strong fortress, called Cimiata. He was 
1 CONC joined by numerous bodies of troops from different quarters and gradually extended his dominions in Pontus and created the foundations for the birth of a new kingdom, which may be judged to have risen about 281 BC when Mithridates
1 CONC  assumed the title of basileus (king).1 In the same year, we find him concluding an alliance with the town of Heraclea Pontica, in Bithynia, to protect them against Seleucus2. At a subsequent period, Mithridates is found acquiring
1 CONC  support from the Gauls (who later settled in Asia Minor) in order to overthrow a force sent against him by Ptolemy, king of Egypt.3 These are the recorded events of his reign, which lasted for thirty-six years.4 He was succeeded 
1 CONC by his son Ariobarzanes. He seems to have been buried in a royal grave near the kingdom's capital, Amasia. Next to him will be buried all the kings of Pontus until the fall of Sinope in 183 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Appian,5 he was eighth in descent from the first satrap of Pontus under Darius the Great and sixth in ascending order from Mithridates Eupator. However, this point is controversial since Plutarch6 writes that eight ge
1 CONC nerations of kings of Pontus stemmed from him before Roman subjection.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Mithridates II of Cius King of Pontus
1 CONT 302 BC – 266 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ariobarzanes
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
