0 @N6000000002187991205@ NOTE Ptolemy XI Alexander II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT     Numbering the Ptolemies is a modern invention; the Greeks distinguished them by nickname. The number given here is the present consensus; but there has been some disagreement about which Ptolemies should be counted as reigning
1 CONC . Older sources may give a number one higher or lower, but the same epithet.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy XI Alexander II was a member of the Ptolemaic dynasty who ruled Egypt for a few days in 80 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy XI was born to Ptolemy X Alexander and either Cleopatra Selene or Berenice III. Ptolemy IX Lathryos died in 81 or 80, leaving no legitimate heir, and so Cleopatra Berenice (another name of Berenice III) ruled alone for a t
1 CONC ime. However, Rome's Sulla wanted a pro-Roman ruler on the throne, and sent the young son of Ptolemy X to Egypt, displaying Ptolemy Alexander's will in Rome as justification for this obvious intervention.
1 CONT
1 CONT The will also required Ptolemy XI to marry Cleopatra Berenice, who was his stepmother (or possibly his natural mother - the ancient sources are unclear). However, nineteen days after the marriage, Ptolemy murdered his bride for un
1 CONC known reasons, an unwise move since Berenice was very popular; Ptolemy was immediately lynched by the citizens of Alexandria.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by Ptolemy XII.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Peter Green, Alexander to Actium (University of California Press, 1990), pp. 553-554 ISBN 0-520-05611-6
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Berenice III Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT with Berenice III Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy XII
0 @N6000000002188198368@ NOTE Heliocles I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT The Greco-Bactrian Heliocles, circ. 145-130 BCE, relative (son or brother) and successor of Eucratides the Great, was probably the last Greek king who reigned over the Bactrian country. His reign was a troubled one. According to R
1 CONC oman historian Justin, Eucratides was murdered by his son and co-ruler, though Justin fails to name the perpetrator. The patricide might have led to instability, even civil war, which caused the Indian parts of the empire to be lo
1 CONC st to Indo-Greek king Menander I.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 130 BCE a nomadic people, the Yuezhi, started to invade Bactria from the north and we could assume that Heliocles was killed in battle during this invasion. Details from Chinese sources seem to indicate that the nomad invasio
1 CONC n did not end civilisation in Bactria entirely. Hellenised cities continued to exist for some time, and the well-organised agricultural systems were not demolished.
1 CONT
1 CONT Even if this was the end of the original Greco-Bactrian kingdom, the Greeks continued to rule in northwestern India to the end of the 1st century BCE, under the Indo-Greek Kingdom. It is unclear whether the dynasty of Eucratides w
1 CONC as extinguished with the death of Heliocles I or if members of the family emigrated eastwards. Several later Indo-Greek king, including Heliocles II, struck coins which could be associated with the dynasty.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eucratides Greco-Bactrian Ruler
1 CONT (Eastern Bactria)
1 CONT (150-130 BCE) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Zoilos I
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
