1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Films
1 CONT  Film poster for the 1917 Cleopatra film.The earliest Cleopatra-related motion picture was Antony and Cleopatra (1908) with Florence Lawrence as Cleopatra. The earliest film on Cleopatra as the main subject was Cleopatra, Queen of
1 CONC  Egypt, starring Helen Gardner (1912).
1 CONT
1 CONT Among the film/TV works inspired by the Queen of the Nile:
1 CONT
1 CONT (1917): Cleopatra: Theda Bara (Cleopatra), Fritz Leiber (Caesar), Thurston Hall (Antony). Directed by J. Gordon Edwards. Based on Émile Moreau's play Cléopatre, Sardou's play Cléopatre, and Shakespeare's Antony and Cleopatra. 
1 CONT (1934): Cleopatra: Claudette Colbert (Cleopatra), Warren William (Caesar), Henry Wilcoxon (Antony). Oscar-winning Cecil B. DeMille epic. 
1 CONT (1946): Caesar and Cleopatra: Vivien Leigh (Cleopatra), Claude Rains (Caesar), Stewart Granger, Flora Robson — Oscar-nominated version of George Bernard Shaw's play. Leigh also played Cleopatra opposite then-husband's Laurence Oli
1 CONC vier's Caesar in a later London stage version. 
1 CONT (1953): Serpent of the Nile: Rhonda Fleming (Cleopatra), Raymond Burr (Mark Antony), Michael Fox (Octavian). 
1 CONT (1963): Cleopatra: Elizabeth Taylor (Cleopatra), Rex Harrison (Caesar), Richard Burton (Antony). Oscar-winning block-buster most (in)famously remembered for the off-screen affair between Taylor and Burton and the at-the-time massi
1 CONC ve $44 million cost. 
1 CONT (1964): Carry On Cleo, a spoof of the 1963 film, with Amanda Barrie as Cleopatra, Sid James as Mark Antony, and Kenneth Williams as Caesar. 
1 CONT (1970): Kureopatora (Cleopatra: Queen of Sex), a bizarre animated Japanese movie by Osamu Tezuka. The English subtitled version is said to be lost. 
1 CONT (1974): Antony & Cleopatra: performed by London's Royal Shakespeare Company. Starred Janet Suzman (Cleopatra), Richard Johnson (Antony), and Patrick Stewart (Enobarbus). 
1 CONT (1999): Cleopatra (movie): Leonor Varela (Cleopatra), Timothy Dalton (Caesar), Billy Zane (Antony). Based on the book Memoirs of Cleopatra by Margaret George, it is less faithful to history than the earlier versions 
1 CONT A longer discussion of Cleopatra films is at: Cleopatra (movie).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT TV
1 CONT (2005): Rome (TV series), episode 8 of season 1 features Cleopatra extensively, along with her brother Ptolemy XIII. The episode begins with Pompey's assassination and ends with the birth of Caesarion. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Opera
1 CONT Appears as a character in operas by Handel, Carl Heinrich Graun, Johann Adolf Hasse, and Johann Mattheson| 
1 CONT Antony and Cleopatra by Samuel Barber opened the new Metropolitan Opera House in 1966. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Ancient art - triumph painting, sculpture
1 CONT The most famous painting of Cleopatra is one that almost certainly no longer exists now. Because the queen died in Egypt well before Augustus' triumph could be put on in Rome, in which she would have walked in chains, Augustus com
1 CONC missioned a large painting of her, which was carried in his triumphal procession, and which may have represented her being poisoned by an asp. The sources for the story are Plut. Ant. 86 and App. Civ. II.102, although the latter m
1 CONC ay well refer to a statue, and Cass. Dio LI.21.3 reports that the "image" was of gold, and thus not a painting at all. The purported painting was seen and engraved in the early 19th century: it was in a private collection near Sor
1 CONC rento. Since then, this painting is said to have formed part of a collection in Cortona, but there no longer appears to be any trace of it; its quiet disappearance is almost certainly due to its being a fake. For comprehensive det
1 CONC ails on the entire question, see the external links at the end of this article.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Paintings, Renaissance onwards
1 CONT Cleopatra and her death have inspired hundreds of paintings from the Renaissance to our own time, none of them of any historical value of course; the subject appealing in particular to French academic painters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Thomas Browne: Of the Picture describing the death of Cleopatra (1672) 
1 CONT John Sartain: On the Antique Portrait of Cleopatra (1818) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT The suicide
1 CONT Suicide of Cleopatra. Oil on canvas. 46 x 36-3/4 in. (116.8 x 93.3 cm) painted by Giovanni Francesco Barbieri, also called Guercino. Painted in 1621 and which hangs in the collection in the Norton Simon Museum in Pasadena, Califor
1 CONC nia. It shows Cleopatra and in her hand a snake that she prepares to use in her suicide. 
1 CONT The Death of Cleopatra, painted by Jean André Rixens, painted in 1874 and that hangs in the Musée des Augustins in Toulouse, France. 
1 CONT  The Death of Cleopatra, Guido Cagnacci, 1658The Death of Cleopatra, painted by Guido Cagnacci, painted in 1658. Oil on canvas. Hanging in the Vienna Kunsthistorisches Museum. 
