0 @N6000000002187718997@ NOTE Alexander II of Epirus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Alexander II, king of Epirus, succeeded his father Pyrrhus in 272 BC. He attacked Antigonus Gonatas and conquered the greater part of Macedonia, but was in turn driven out of both Epirus and Macedonia by Demetrius, the son of Anti
1 CONC gonus. He subsequently recovered his kingdom by the aid of the Acarnanians and Aetolians. He died about 260 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT See Thirlwall, History of Greece, vol. viii.; Droysen, Hellenismus; B. Niese, Geschichte der griechischen und makedonischen Staaten; J. Beloch, Griech. Gesch. vol. iii.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000002187718819@ NOTE Zipoites I of Bithynia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Zipoites I (in Greek Z?po?t?? or Z?ßo?t??; ruled c. 326–278 BC) was the second independent ruler of Bithynia. He succeeded his father Bas on the throne in 326 BC and reigned for forty-eight years carrying on successful wars with L
1 CONC ysimachus and Antiochus, the son of Seleucus.1 In 315 BC he carried on a war against Astakos and Chalcedon, which failed for the relief sent by Antigonus' troops.2 He founded a city which was called Zipoition after him at the foot
1 CONC  of Mount Lypedron; the exact location of both the city and the mountain in unknown. He lived to the age of seventy-six, and left behind him four children, the eldest of whom, Nicomedes, succeeded him.3 He was the first ruler of B
1 CONC ithyinia to assume the title of basileus (king), action he seems to have done in 297 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bas King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Nicomedes I 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Cohen, Getzel M.; The Hellenistic Settlements in Europe, the Islands and Asia Minor (1996), "Zipoition" 
1 CONT Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Ziboetes", Boston, (1867) 
0 @N6000000000437154160@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Emperor Of Rome
0 @N6000000002187712847@ NOTE Julius Caesar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gaius Julius Caesar 
1 CONT Dictator of the Roman Republic 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign October, 49 BC–March 15, 44 BC 
1 CONT Born July 12 or July 13, 100 BC 
1 CONT  Rome 
1 CONT Died March 15, 44 BC 
1 CONT  Rome 
1 CONT Successor Augustus (as Roman Emperor) 
1 CONT Consort 1) Cornelia Cinna minor 84 BC–68 BC
1 CONT 2) Pompeia Sulla 68 BC–63 BC
1 CONT 3) Calpurnia Pisonis 59 BC–44 BC 
1 CONT Issue Julia Caesaris 
1 CONT Royal House Julio-Claudian 
1 CONT Father Gaius Julius Caesar the Elder 
1 CONT Mother Aurelia Cotta 
1 CONT
1 CONT Gaius Julius Caesar (IPA: ['ga?ius 'ju?lius 'kaisar];[1]), July 12 or July 13, 100 BC – March 15, 44 BC) was a Roman military and political leader and one of the most influential men in world history. He played a critical role in 
1 CONC the transformation of the Roman Republic into the Roman Empire. His conquest of Gaul extended the Roman world all the way to the Atlantic Ocean, and he was also responsible for the first Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC. Caesar 
1 CONC was widely considered to be one of the foremost military geniuses of his time, as well as a brilliant politician and one of the ancient world's strongest leaders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leading his legions across the Rubicon, Caesar sparked civil war in 49 BC that left him the undisputed master of the Roman world. After assuming control of the government, he began extensive reforms of Roman society and government
1 CONC . He was proclaimed dictator for life, and he heavily centralized the bureaucracy of the Republic. This forced the hand of a friend of Caesar, Marcus Junius Brutus, who then conspired with others to murder the dictator and restore
1 CONC  the Republic. This dramatic assassination occurred on the Ides of March (March 15th) in 44 BC and led to another Roman civil war. In 42 BC, two years after his assassination, the Roman Senate officially sanctified him as one of t
1 CONC he Roman deities.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar's military campaigns are known in detail from his own written Commentaries (Commentarii), and many details of his life are recorded by later historians, such as Appian, Suetonius, Plutarch, Cassius Dio and Strabo. Other inf
1 CONC ormation can be gleaned from other contemporary sources, such as the letters and speeches of Caesar's political rival Cicero, the poetry of Catullus and the writings of the historian Sallust.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 1.1 Early life 
