0 @N6000000002187624024@ NOTE Alexander Helios
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander Helios (Greek: ? ????a?d??? ?????, 25 December 40 BC - Between 29 BC - 25 BC) was a Ptolemaic Prince and was the eldest son to Greek Ptolemaic Queen Cleopatra VII of Egypt and Roman Triumvir Mark Antony. His younger twin
1 CONC  was Ptolemaic Princess Cleopatra Selene (II). He is of Greek and Roman heritage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra named him Alexander, in honor of her Greek Macedonian heritage and after her maternal grandfather, who had the name Alexander. Various Pharaohs of the Ptolemaic Dynasty, had sons who had the name Alexander. According to 
1 CONC Plutarch (Antony, clause 36), his second name in Ancient Greek means ‘Sun’ and the historian explains that his second name was the counterpart of his twin sister’s second name Selene ‘Moon’.
1 CONT
1 CONT He born, raised and educated in Alexandria, Egypt. In late 34 BC, at the Donations of Alexandria, he was made ruler of Armenia, Media and Parthia. Others, who were, already occupied these three kingdoms: Artavasdes II of Armenia (
1 CONC he was captured by Mark Antony), Artavasdes I of Media and Phraates IV of Parthia. His parents probably wanted him to succeed these thrones overtime. In 33 BC, for unknown political reasons, he was engaged to Iotapa, a Princess of
1 CONC  Media and daughter of King Artavasdes I of Media.
1 CONT
1 CONT His parents were defeated by Octavian (future Roman Emperor Caesar Augustus) during the naval battle at Actium, Greece in 31 BC. The next year, his parents committed suicide as Octavian and his army invaded Egypt. The three childr
1 CONC en became orphans. Iotapa left Egypt, to return to her father. Her fate is unknown, afterwards.
1 CONT
1 CONT Octavian took him, his sister and his brother Ptolemy Philadelphus (Cleopatra) from Egypt to Italy. Octavian celebrated his military triumph in Rome, by parading the three orphans in heavy golden chains in the streets of Rome. The
1 CONC  chains were so heavy, they couldn’t walk. The three siblings were taken by Octavian and given to Octavia Minor to be raised in her house in Rome. Octavia Minor became their guardian, was also Octavian’s second elder sister and th
1 CONC eir father’s formal wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT The fate of Alexander Helios is unknown. Augustus most probably didn’t murder him, because he spared his life. Plutarch states, that the only child that Octavian killed out of Antony’s children was Marcus Antonius Antyllus. Nothin
1 CONC g is mentioned about any military service, political career, if he was involved in any scandals, any marriage plans or any descendants. If he survived to adulthood, it would have been mentioned in the ancient sources. Alexander mo
1 CONC st probably died through illness in Rome, through the climate change of the two countries. This is the most reasonable possibility and the logical choice.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT http://www.tyndale.cam.ac.uk/Egypt/ptolemies/helios.htm 
1 CONT http://www.ruark.org/coins/Mauretania/ 
1 CONT Plutarch - Antony 
0 @N6000000002187823833@ NOTE Cleopatra Selene (II)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra Selene II (Greek: ? ??e?pàt?a Se????) (25 December 40 BC - 6 AD), also known as Cleopatra VIII of Egypt was a Ptolemaic Princess and was an only daughter to Greek Ptolemaic Queen Cleopatra VII of Egypt and Roman Triumvir
1 CONC  Mark Antony. She is the younger twin to Ptolemaic Prince Alexander Helios and was the namesake of her mother. According to Plutarch (Antony - clause 36), her second name in Ancient Greek means ‘Moon‘. She is of Greek and Roman He
1 CONC ritage. Cleopatra was born, raised and educated in Alexandria, Egypt. In late 34 BC, during the Donations of Alexandria, she was made ruler of Cyrenaica and Libya.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her parents, were defeated by Octavian (future Roman Emperor Caesar Augustus) during the naval battle at Actium, Greece in 31 BC. The next year, her parents committed suicide as Octavian and his army invaded Egypt. Cleopatra and h
1 CONC er brothers became orphans.
1 CONT
1 CONT Octavian took the three orphans from Egypt to Italy. Octavian celebrated his military triumph in Rome, by parading the three orphans in heavy golden chains in the streets of Rome. The chains were so heavy, they couldn’t walk. The 
1 CONC three siblings were taken by Octavian and given to Octavia Minor to be raised in her house in Rome. Octavia Minor became their guardian, was also Octavian’s second elder sister and their father’s formal wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Augustus between 26 BC - 20 BC, arranged for Cleopatra to marry African King Juba II of Numidia in Rome. The emperor as a wedding present gave her a huge dowry and appointed her Queen of Numidia. In return, she became an ally to R
1 CONC ome. By then her brothers (Alexander Helios and Ptolemy Philadelphus (Cleopatra)), died probably through illness or murder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Juba and Cleopatra returned to Numidia and didn’t rule there for long. The local Numidians disapproved that Juba became too Romanized and this caused civil unrest. This forced the couple to leave Numidia and move to Mauretania. Th
1 CONC ey renamed their new capital Iol to Caesaria (modern Cherchell, Algeria).
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra is said to have exercised great influence on policies that Juba created. Through her influence, the Mauretanian Kingdom flourished, particularly with Mauretania exporting and trading well throughout the Mediterranean. Th
1 CONC e construction and sculptural projects (including the mausoleum where the couple are buried) at Caesaria and at another city Volubillis, were built and display a rich mixture of Ancient Egyptian, Greek and Roman architectural styl
1 CONC es.
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Cleopatra and Juba are:
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra of Mauretania, a possible daughter 
1 CONT Ptolemy of Mauretania (1 BC - 40 AD) 
1 CONT Drusilla of Mauretania (born 5 AD) 
1 CONT Unfortunately, there is no surviving written sources on Cleopatra’s life. According to numismatic evidence and surviving monuments, suggest that Cleopatra inherited the iron will and perseverance of the Ptolemaic women. Cleopatra 
1 CONC seemed to have been religious; patriotic of her Egyptian and Greek heritage; thus ignoring her Roman heritage and Cleopatra wanted to retain and continue the Ptolemaic Legacy.
1 CONT
1 CONT An epigram by Krinagorasis, is thought to be an eulogy of Cleopatra’s: The moon herself grew dark, rising at sunset, Covering her suffering in the night, Because she saw her beautiful namesake, Selene, Breathless, descending to Ha
1 CONC des, With her she had had the beauty of her light in common, And mingled her own darkness with her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Zenobia of Palmyra is descended from her. Cleopatra is mentioned in the novels by Robert Graves, I Claudius and Claudius the God.
0 @N6000000002187723269@ NOTE Ptolemy Philadelphus (Cleopatra)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy Philadelphus (Greek: ? ?t??eµa??? F???de?f??, August/September 36 BC - 29 BC) was a Ptolemaic Prince and was the youngest child of Greek Ptolemaic Queen Cleopatra VII of Egypt and Roman Triumvir Mark Antony. Ptolemy was of
1 CONC  Greek and Roman heritage. He was born in Antioch, Turkey. Ptolemy was named after the original Ptolemy II Philadelphus (the second Pharaoh of the Ptolemaic Dynasty) and Cleopatra’s intention was recreating the formal Ptolemaic Ki
1 CONC ngdom. In late 34 BC, at the Donations of Alexandria, Ptolemy was made ruler of Syria, Phoenicia and Cilicia.
1 CONT
1 CONT His parents were defeated by Octavian (future Roman Emperor Caesar Augustus) during the naval battle at Actium, Greece in 31 BC. The next year, his parents committed suicide as Octavian and his army invaded Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Octavian took him and elder siblings Alexander Helios and Cleopatra Selene (II) from Egypt to Italy. The three children became orphans. Octavian celebrated his military triumph in Rome, by parading the three orphans in heavy golde
1 CONC n chains in the streets of Rome. The chains were so heavy, they couldn’t walk. The three siblings were taken by Octavian and given to Octavia Minor to be raised in her house in Rome. Octavia Minor became their guardian, was also O
1 CONC ctavian’s second elder sister and their father’s formal wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT The fate of Ptolemy Philadelphus is unknown. Augustus most probably didn’t murder him, because he spared his life. Plutarch states, that the only child that Octavian killed out of Antony’s children was Marcus Antonius Antyllus. No
1 CONC thing is mentioned about any military service, political career, if he was involved in any scandals, any marriage plans or any descendants. If he survived to adulthood, it would have been mentioned in the ancient sources. Ptolemy 
1 CONC most probably died from illness in the winter of 29 BC, being unused to the Italian climate.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT http://www.tyndale.cam.ac.uk/Egypt/ptolemies/philadelphus.htm 
1 CONT http://www.ruark.org/coins/Mauretania/ 
1 CONT Plutarch - Antony 
