0 @N6000000002187720083@ NOTE Menon of Pharsalus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Menon (in Greek Me???; died 321 BC) was a citizen of Pharsalus in Thessaly, and a man of great influence and reputation, took a prominent part in the Lamian war, and commanded the Thessalian cavalry in the battle with the Macedoni
1 CONC ans, in which Leonnatus was slain. Plutarch tells us that his services were highly valued by the confederates, and that he held a place in their estimation second only to Leosthenes, At the battle of Crannon (322 BC), he and Antip
1 CONC hilus, the Athenian, were defeated by Antipater and Craterus, though the Thessalian horse under his command maintained in the action its superiority over that of the enemy; and they felt themselves compelled to open a negotiation 
1 CONC with the conquerors, which led to the dissolution of the Greek confederacy. But when Antipater was obliged to cross over to Asia against Perdiccas, the Aetolians renewed the war, and were zealously seconded in Thessaly by Menon, t
1 CONC hrough whose influence it probably was that most of the Thessalian towns were induced to take part in the insurrection. Soon after, however, he was defeated by Polyperchon in a pitched battle, in which he himself was slain, 321 BC
1 CONC . His daughter Phthia he gave in marriage to Aeacides, king of Epirus, by whom she became the mother of Pyrrhus.1
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Menon (4)", Boston, (1867) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Note
1 CONT 1 Diodorus Siculus, Bibliotheca, xviii. 15, 17, 38; Plutarch, Parallel Lives, "Pyrrhus", 1, "Phocion", 24, 25 
0 @N6000000002187720055@ NOTE Arymbas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Arymbas (or Arybas, Arybbas) was the king of Epirus, succeeding his brother Neoptolemos in the year 360 BC. Both Arymbas and Neoptolemos claimed to be descendants of another Neoptolemos, the son of Achilles. Arymbas was the father
1 CONC  of Aeacides of Epirus by Troas and the son of Alcetas II of Epirus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arymbas made a treaty of alliance with Philip of Macedon, which was cemented by a marriage between Philip and Neoptolemos' daughter, Olympias. Olympias later became known as the mother of Alexander the Great.
0 @N6000000002187718837@ NOTE Bas of Bithynia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bas (in Greek Ba?; ruled 376–326 BC), first indipendent ruler of Bithynia, governed fifty years, from 376 to 326 BC, and died at the age of 71. He succeeded his father Boteiras, and was himself succeeded by his own son Zipoites. H
1 CONC e defeated Calas, a general of Alexander the Great, and maintained the independence of Bithynia.1
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Boteiras Prince of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Zipoites I 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Bas", Boston, (1867) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Note
1 CONT 1 Memnon, History of Heracleia, 12 
0 @N6000000002187599711@ NOTE Archelaus of Cappadocia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Archelaus (died 17) was the last king of Cappadocia. He was the great-grandson of Archelaus, a general in the First and Third Mithridatic Wars.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 36 BC, Archelaus was made king by Mark Antony, whom, however, he deserted after the Battle of Actium. Octavian enlarged his kingdom by the addition of part of Cilicia and Lesser Armenia. He was not popular with his subjects, wh
1 CONC o even brought an accusation against him in Rome, on which occasion he was defended by Tiberius. Subsequently he was accused by Tiberius, when emperor, of endeavouring to stir up a revolution, and died in confinement in Rome. Capp
1 CONC adocia was then made a Roman province.
1 CONT
1 CONT Archelaus was said to have been the author of a geographical work, and to have written treatises On Stones and Rivers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ariarathes X King of Cappadocia
1 CONT 36 BC–17 AD Succeeded by:
1 CONT — 
