1 CONT Darius I Great King of Media and Persia
1 CONT 486–465 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes I 
1 CONT King of Egypt
1 CONT 486–465 BC 
0 @N6000000002188198778@ NOTE Roxana
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Roxana (Bactrian: Roshanak; literally "little shiny star" or "light"), was a Bactrian noble and a wife of Alexander the Great. She was born earlier than the year 341 BC, though the precise date remains uncertain. She was the daugh
1 CONC ter of a Bactrian named Oxyartes of Balkh in Bactria (then eastern Persia, now northern Afghanistan), and married Alexander in 327 BC after he captured her when the fortress of Sogdian Rock surrendered to him. Balkh was the last o
1 CONC f the Persian Empire's provinces to fall to Alexander, and the marriage was an attempt to reconcile the Bactrian satrapies to Alexander's rule, although ancient sources describe Alexander's professed love for her. Roxana accompani
1 CONC ed him on his campaign in India in 326 BC. She bore him a posthumous son called Alexander IV Aegus, after Alexander's sudden death at Babylon in 323 BC. With the king's death, Roxana and her son became victims of the political int
1 CONC rigues of the collapse of the Alexandrian empire. Roxana murdered Alexander's other widow, Stateira, and Stateira's sister Drypteis (Pl. Alex. 77.4). Roxana and her son were protected by Alexander's mother, Olympias, in Macedon, b
1 CONC ut her assassination in 316 BC allowed Cassander to seek kingship. Since Alexander IV Aegus was the legitimate heir to the Alexandrian empire, Cassander ordered him and Roxana assassinated around 309 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Portrayal in film
1 CONT
1 CONT In Oliver Stone's 2004 biopic Alexander, Rosario Dawson portrayed Roxana, opposite Colin Farrell as Alexander the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Livius.org: Roxane by Jona Lendering
1 CONT     * Wiki Classical Dictionary: Roxane, daughter of Oxyartes
