0 @N6000000002187597423@ NOTE Osroene
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Osrhoene)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Osroene (also: Osrohene, Osrhoene; Syriac: ?????? ???? ???? ?????), also known by the name of its capital city, Edessa (modern Sanli Urfa, in Syriac: ?????), was one of several kingdoms arising from the dissolution of the Seleucid
1 CONC  Empire. The Syriac kingdom occupied an area on what is now the border between Syria and Turkey.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was in this region that the "legend of Abgar" originated, for which see Abgarus of Edessa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osroene was absorbed into the Roman Empire in 114 as a semi-autonomous vassal state, then incorporated as a province in 214.
0 @N6000000002187597388@ NOTE Ruled 80-110
0 @N6000000002187614275@ NOTE Hystaspes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Hystaspes (Greek form of Old Persian Vishtaspa) can refer to two individuals:
1 CONT
1 CONT A semi-legendary Vishtasp (modern Persian ?????? Goshtâsb) or "ancient poet and leader" praised by Zoroaster as his protector and a true believer, the son of Aurvataspa (Persian Lohrasp). The later tradition and the Shahnama of Fi
1 CONC rdousi makes him (in the modern form of his name, Key Gushtasp) a "king of Iran". Since Zoroaster probably preached his religion in what is now eastern Iran, Vishtaspa must have been a dynast in Bactria or Sogdiana. 
1 CONT A Persian, son of Arsames, king of Persia, and father of Darius I, great king of Media and Persia. He was satrap of Parthia under the rule of Cambyses II, Smerdis and his own son. Darius himself mentions this in the Behistun inscr
1 CONC iption (2. 65). 
1 CONT Ammianus Marcellinus (xxiii. 6. 32) and many modern authors have identified the father of Darius with the protector of Zoroaster. This identification is not universally accepted and whether it is deemed possible depends on the dat
1 CONC e one assigns to Zoroaster.
0 @N6000000002187604033@ NOTE Cyrus the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cyrus II of Persia 
1 CONT 576 or 590 BC — July 529 BC 
1 CONT
1 CONT An old Iranian portrait of Cyrus the Great (artist's concept). 
1 CONT Place of birth: Presumably Anshan 
1 CONT Place of death: Unknown; along the Syr Darya 
1 CONT
1 CONT Cyrus the Great (Old Persian: Kuruš,[1] modern Persian: ?????, Kourosh; ca. 576 or 590 BC — July 529 BC), also known as Cyrus II of Persia and Cyrus the Elder, was the founder of the Persian Empire under the Achaemenid dynasty. As
1 CONC  the ruler of the Persian people in Anshan, from approximately 559 BC, he conquered the Medes and unified the two separate Iranian kingdoms. He then expanded beyond the Iranian plateau, conquering most of Southwest Asia to create 
1 CONC the largest empire the world had yet seen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little certain is known, however, of his actual life or reign. He is best known for the stories told about him later and by foreigners, which range from depicting him as the exemplar of a princely education, or as the fulfillment 
1 CONC of prophecy, to earning a death of bloodthirsty arrogance. He is perhaps most remembered for restoring the exiled Jews to Jerusalem.
1 CONT
