0 @N6000000001503791159@ NOTE Weis' "Ancestral Roots" (190:3).
1 CONT
1 CONT Known by the nickname "the lame", he was one of several frankish
1 CONT
1 CONT kings, his kingdom being in the Rhineland. When CLOVIS (RIN 2218) was
1 CONT
1 CONT consolidating his power as sole king of the Franks, Sigebert was
1 CONT
1 CONT probably his most powerful rival. It appears that the two king were on
1 CONT
1 CONT friendly terms. Sigebert's son, CLODERIC, even faught with CLOVIS in his
1 CONT
1 CONT campaign against the Visigoths. As related by Edward James in his book,
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Franks" : "CLOVIS persuaded CLODERIC to kill his father as he was
1 CONT
1 CONT walking in the forest outside Cologne. While CLODERIC was selecting his
1 CONT
1 CONT reward from his father's treasure chest, one of CLOVIS' Franks split his
1 CONT
1 CONT skull with an axe. CLOVIS announced to the Rhineland Franks that
1 CONT
1 CONT CLODERIC had killed his father and had in turn been killed; they clashed
1 CONT
1 CONT their shields, shouted their approval, and made CLOVIS their king."
1 CONT
1 CONT As the Merovingian dynasty developed, the separate identities of
1 CONT
1 CONT Neustria (home of the Salian Franks) and Austrasia (home of the
1 CONT
1 CONT Ripuarian Franks) was maintained. 
